var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"The works cited in these docs and docstrings.\n\nFörstner, W. and Gülch, E. (1987). A Fast Operator for Detection and Precise Location of Distinct Points, Corners and Centres of Circular Features. In: Proceedings of the ISPRS Intercommission Conference on Fast Processing of Photogrammetric Data (Interlaken, Switzerland); pp. 281–305.\n\n\n\nKöthe, U. (2003). Edge and Junction Detection with an Improved Structure Tensor. In: Pattern Recognition, 25th DAGM Symposium, Vol. 2781 of Lecture Notes in Computer Science (Springer, Berlin, Heidelberg); pp. 25–32.\n\n\n\nLuxen, M. (2005). Interest Point Detector by Förstner/Gülch (Förstner-Operator), MATLAB implementation ip_fop.m, version 1.0. Department of Photogrammetry, Institute of Geodesy and Geoinformation, University of Bonn; contact Thomas Läbe. Dated 13.01.2005, with changes from 10.03.2011.\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api/cache/#Cache-and-Visualization","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"FileCache needs the JLD2 extension (using JLD2), and detections! and the image viewer compare_images need a Makie backend.","title":"Cache and Visualization"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.CACHE_FORMAT","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"CACHE_FORMAT\n\nThe layout version of the values a FileCache stores, part of every cache_key. The backend (JLD2) stores Julia types, so a cached value whose type's layout changes cannot be read back as the new type: raising this number makes every earlier entry a miss (recomputed), never a load that fails. Raise it with any change to the layout of a cached type.\n\n1: the initial layout.\n2 (2026-11): VisualGeometryToolkitCore's CovMat / PrecMat hold a static matrix (CovMat{N, T, M, C}; was CovMat{N, T, L, C}), so cached uncertain detections of the earlier layout do not load.\n3 (2026-11): LinearRaw{Mode, I, S, G, M} holds its image and saturation arrays as the mosaic's device made them (was LinearRaw{Mode, G, M}), so cached decodes of the earlier layout do not load.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.CACHE_FORMAT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.FileCache","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"FileCache{D}\n\nWrapper around an open file-backed key-value store. The concrete backend D is determined by the extension (e.g., JLD2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.FileCache"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.cache_key-Tuple","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"cache_key(args...) -> String\n\nCompute a stable hex-encoded SHA-256 cache key from arbitrary arguments and the cache's layout version (CACHE_FORMAT): an entry written under an earlier layout is a miss. The caller decides what else goes in; this function just hashes.\n\nExample\n\nkey = cache_key(board_uid, model_hash, cutoff, patch_size)\nget!(cache, key) do ... end\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.cache_key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.close_cache-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"close_cache(cache)\n\nClose the cache file handle. No-op for nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.close_cache"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.open_cache-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"open_cache(path) -> FileCache or nothing\n\nOpen a persistent cache file. Returns nothing if path is nothing or the file cannot be opened (logs a warning).\n\nRequires a backend extension (e.g., using JLD2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.open_cache"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.compare_images","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"compare_images(entries; titles = nothing, default = first name, size = (1800, 1000)) -> Figure\n\nThe stack's one image viewer: a menu over named entries, each a tuple of images to compare (a pair, typically) or a function returning one (called when the entry is chosen, so a large set loads lazily), drawn on one axis per image with the axes linked (they pan and zoom together, pixel for pixel). Every image is drawn by VisualGeometryToolkitCore's orientation rule (imshow!: x right, y down, COLMAP pixels, row 1 at the top), whatever its element type (a srgb_image display image, a real matrix through a colormap). entries is a vector of name => images pairs, every entry the same number of images; titles names the axes (one per image), and default the entry shown first. Display it with a Makie backend (using GLMakie; display(fig)). An empty entries, a default not among them or a title count other than the images' is an ArgumentError.\n\nRequires Makie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.compare_images"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detections!","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"detections!(ax, dets::AbstractVector{<:Detection};\n            cutoff=1.0, color_by=nothing, colormap=:viridis, kwargs...)\n\nOverlay detections as batched ellipse boundaries (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's ellipses!).\n\ncolor_by selects the stroke coloring:\n\nnothing — constant strokecolor\n:response — |metadata.response| through colormap\nan AbstractVector{<:Real} — explicit per-detection values\n\nRequires Makie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detections!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/matching/#Matching","page":"Matching","text":"Strategies, metrics and matches; the matchers on the CPU, on a GPU and over stacked galleries; described features; the LoMa networks.","title":"Matching"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.nn_match","page":"Matching","text":"nn_match(desc1, desc2, strategy = MutualNN(); device = default_device(), kw...) -> Vector{NNMatch}\n\nmatch on device, any KernelAbstractions backend (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's default_device() when none is supplied: an accelerator when one is functional, else KA.CPU()). Every backend runs the same GEMM and selection kernels, so a GPU (e.g. CUDABackend()) returns what the CPU returns, with every keyword match accepts: metric, dm, ratio_threshold, min_similarity, max_distance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.nn_match"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.CspondSet","page":"Matching","text":"CspondSet(features::AbstractVector)\nCspondSet(features::Tuple)\nCspondSet(T::Type)\n\nAn N-way correspondence: the same scene point observed as one feature in each of several images, where Cspond is the two-way case. It wraps a vector of features and iterates, indexes and has a length like one. A tuple becomes a fixed-size SVector; a Vector (or CspondSet(T), an empty one of element type T) grows with push! and append!.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> track = CspondSet(Int);\n\njulia> push!(track, 3); push!(track, 7);\n\njulia> length(track), track[2], collect(track)\n(2, 7, [3, 7])\n\njulia> length(CspondSet((1, 2, 3)))\n3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.CspondSet"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.CosineSimilarity","page":"Matching","text":"CosineSimilarity <: DescriptorSimilarity\n\nCosine similarity for L2-NORMALIZED descriptors, computed as the dot product a·b — no normalization is applied, so un-normalized input gives raw dot products. Range [-1, 1] for unit vectors, 1 = identical.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.CosineSimilarity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorDistance","page":"Matching","text":"DescriptorDistance <: DescriptorMetric\n\nDistance metrics: lower values = more similar. NN finds minima.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorDistance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorMetric","page":"Matching","text":"DescriptorMetric\n\nAbstract type for descriptor comparison metrics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorMetric"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorSimilarity","page":"Matching","text":"DescriptorSimilarity <: DescriptorMetric\n\nSimilarity metrics: higher values = more similar. NN finds maxima.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorSimilarity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.KnnMatch","page":"Matching","text":"KnnMatch(k) <: NNStrategy\n\nk-nearest-neighbor matching: for each desc1 row, keep its k nearest desc2 columns instead of only the single best. Permissive by design — surfaces true matches that sit at rank 2..k or just under a strict cutoff (common with repetitive patterns, where a confuser can out-rank the true neighbor). Downstream geometry (e.g. RANSAC) disambiguates the extra candidates. Pair with min_similarity to bound the kept scores.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.KnnMatch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.L2","page":"Matching","text":"L2 <: DescriptorDistance\n\nEuclidean distance: ‖a - b‖. Range [0, 2] for unit vectors, 0 = identical.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.L2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.MutualNN","page":"Matching","text":"MutualNN <: NNStrategy\n\nMutual nearest-neighbor matching: find NN in both directions, keep only pairs where both directions agree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.MutualNN"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NN","page":"Matching","text":"NN <: NNStrategy\n\nOne-way nearest-neighbor matching: for each descriptor in desc1, find the nearest neighbor in desc2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NN"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNMatch","page":"Matching","text":"NNMatch\n\nA nearest-neighbor match: Attributed{Cspond{Int,Int}, NNMatchMeta}.\n\nProperty forwarding gives direct access:\n\nm.first — index into desc1\nm.second — index into desc2\nm.score — match quality (similarity or distance, depending on metric)\nm.ratio — Lowe's SNN ratio\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNMatch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNMatchMeta","page":"Matching","text":"NNMatchMeta\n\nMetadata for a nearest-neighbor match.\n\nFields\n\nscore::Float32: Match quality in the metric's native units (similarity for CosineSimilarity, distance for L2)\nratio::Float32: Lowe's ratio — d_best / d_2nd for a distance metric, s_2nd / s_best for a similarity, so that smaller = more distinctive whatever the metric. Inf32 when there is no second neighbour, and when a similarity's best score is non-positive: the ratio is undefined there, and no ratio test admits it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNMatchMeta"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNStrategy","page":"Matching","text":"NNStrategy\n\nAbstract type for nearest-neighbor matching strategies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNStrategy"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.csponds-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Attributed{Pair{Int64, Int64}, NNMatchMeta}}}","page":"Matching","text":"csponds(features1, features2, matches::AbstractVector{NNMatch})\n\nConvert index-based NN matches to geometric correspondences.\n\nReturns a columnar Csponds whose endpoints are the matched features and whose attribute columns carry BOTH match statistics:\n\nscore::Vector{Float64} — higher-is-better sampling prior (similarity metrics are used as is; distance metrics are inverted 1/(d + 1e-6))\nratio::Vector{Float32} — the Lowe's-ratio value of each match (previously computed and then dropped; it now survives to downstream consumers)\n\nAccess endpoints via sources/targets, attributes via cs.score / cs.ratio; subsetting keeps all columns aligned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.csponds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match","page":"Matching","text":"match(desc1, desc2, [strategy]; metric, dm, ratio_threshold,\n      min_similarity, max_distance) -> Vector{NNMatch}\n\nMatch descriptors using nearest-neighbor search, on the CPU. It runs the same KernelAbstractions matcher as nn_match on a GPU, so a device returns what this returns. Descriptors are compared in their own float type.\n\nPublic but not exported, because it would shadow Base.match: import it (using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures: match) or call VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match.\n\nArguments\n\ndesc1::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: N1×D descriptor matrix (rows = descriptors)\ndesc2::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: N2×D descriptor matrix\nstrategy::NNStrategy: MutualNN() (default), NN() or KnnMatch(k)\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nmetric::DescriptorMetric: CosineSimilarity() (default) or L2()\ndm: Precomputed N1×N2 pairwise matrix (skip pairwise_matrix computation)\nratio_threshold: Lowe's SNN ratio threshold; nothing = disabled\nmin_similarity: Minimum score for DescriptorSimilarity metrics\nmax_distance: Maximum score for DescriptorDistance metrics\n\nReturns\n\nVector{NNMatch} sorted by score (best first).\n\nUsage\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures: match, pairwise_matrix, MutualNN, NN, CosineSimilarity, L2\n\n# Cosine similarity (default) — good for L2-normalized descriptors\nmatch(d1, d2, MutualNN())\nmatch(d1, d2, MutualNN(); min_similarity=0.7)\n\n# L2 distance\nmatch(d1, d2, MutualNN(); metric=L2(), max_distance=0.8)\n\n# Precomputed distance matrix\ndm = pairwise_matrix(CosineSimilarity(), d1, d2)\nmatch(d1, d2, MutualNN(); dm)\n\n# With Lowe's ratio test\nmatch(d1, d2, NN(); ratio_threshold=0.8)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.resolve_one_to_one!-Tuple{AbstractVector{Bool}, AbstractVector{Bool}, Any}","page":"Matching","text":"resolve_one_to_one!(used_a::AbstractVector{Bool}, used_b::AbstractVector{Bool},\n                    candidates; a = c -> c.first, b = c -> c.second) -> Vector{Int}\n\nGreedy one-to-one reduction over an ALREADY-ORDERED candidate list: visit candidates in the given order, keep a candidate when neither of its endpoints is used yet, and mark both endpoints used. Returns the kept indices into candidates (in visit order); used_a/used_b are mutated in place, so pre-seeded entries (e.g. matches certified by an earlier stage) are respected and callers can chain several candidate lists through one pair of masks.\n\na/b extract the two endpoint indices from a candidate — the defaults serve NNMatch (m.first/m.second); supply accessors for tuple lists.\n\nThe caller owns the ORDER: sort best-first (descending similarity, ascending distance) for best-first assignment (the reduction MutualNN sets already satisfy, and the one KnnMatch(k) sets need), by overlap for geometric association, etc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.resolve_one_to_one!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.resolve_one_to_one-Tuple{AbstractVector{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Attributed{Pair{Int64, Int64}, NNMatchMeta}}, Int64, Int64}","page":"Matching","text":"resolve_one_to_one(matches::AbstractVector{NNMatch}, n_a, n_b;\n                   metric = CosineSimilarity()) -> Vector{NNMatch}\n\nBest-first one-to-one reduction of an NNMatch list: visit the best match first and keep each first/second index at most once. metric must be the metric the matches were scored with, because it decides what \"best\" means: the highest score for a DescriptorSimilarity (the default, CosineSimilarity()), the lowest for a DescriptorDistance such as L2(). n_a/n_b bound the two index spaces. MutualNN output is returned unchanged (already one-to-one); KnnMatch(k) output collapses to its best consistent assignment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.resolve_one_to_one"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._nn_select-Tuple{NNStrategy, DescriptorMetric, KernelAbstractions.Backend, Vector{Int64}, Vector{Int64}}","page":"Matching","text":"_nn_select(strategy, metric, dev, offsets, sizes; gal, feat, S, chunk_bytes)\n    -> (top_idx, top_sc, rev_idx)\n\nThe selection behind every match, on backend dev. The gallery is the rows, cut into blocks (offsets, 0-based, and sizes); the scores are either computed from descriptors already on dev (gal, rows × D, and feat, features × D, in the score type) a row range at a time, or read from a precomputed matrix S (rows × features). Returns host arrays: per row the top k feature indices and their signed scores (rows × k), and for MutualNN each feature's best row within each block (features × blocks, block-local), else nothing.\n\nThe score block is the pipeline's largest single allocation (M × N: 3.09 GiB for a 60k gallery against 13.7k detections, 9.79 GiB against 43.5k). Materialising it whole is what OOMs on cluttered frames, so it is built _row_chunks at a time and each block released eagerly via VisualGeometryToolkitCore's free_device_buffers! rather than waiting for a finalizer. The result does not depend on the chunking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._nn_select"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._row_chunks-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Int64, Int64, Type{T}, Integer}} where T","page":"Matching","text":"_row_chunks(backend, M, N, T, chunk_bytes) -> Vector{Tuple{Int, Int}}\n\nConsecutive ROW ranges of an M-row gallery whose score block (rows × N of T) fits the budget. chunk_bytes > 0 is honoured verbatim; otherwise the budget is _SCORE_BUDGET of device_free_bytes, which returns typemax on backends exposing no query — yielding a single chunk, the historical behaviour.\n\nRows, not whole galleries: one calibration target with thousands of blobs is ONE gallery whose block alone can exceed the card (9.3 GiB against a 40k-feature frame, 2026-09-15), so a gallery may span several chunks. That is still exact — the forward sweep is per row, and the reverse sweep merges a gallery's per-column best across its chunks (_ka_top1_cols_blocked!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._row_chunks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.pairwise_matrix","page":"Matching","text":"pairwise_matrix(metric, desc1, desc2, device = default_device()) -> Matrix\n\nThe N1×N2 pairwise comparison matrix: a·b for CosineSimilarity(), ‖a − b‖ for L2(), in the descriptors' float type (Float32 descriptors give Float32, Float64 give Float64). The GEMM runs on device, any KernelAbstractions backend (CUDABackend(), …; VisualGeometryToolkitCore's default_device() when none is supplied: an accelerator when one is functional, else KA.CPU()); the matrix is returned on the host. One implementation serves every backend: there is no separate host method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.pairwise_matrix"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.StackedDescriptorIndex","page":"Matching","text":"StackedDescriptorIndex(backend, galleries::AbstractDict)\n\nDevice-resident stack of descriptor galleries: all galleries' descriptor rows in one matrix, plus per-gallery offsets/sizes for the block-local kernels. galleries maps keys to descriptor matrices (rows = features) or to anything descriptor_matrix accepts. The stack keeps the galleries' float type (Float32 galleries stack as Float32, Float64 as Float64). Build once, reuse across nn_match_stacked calls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.StackedDescriptorIndex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.nn_match_stacked-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{NNStrategy, AbstractDict{K}, Any}} where K","page":"Matching","text":"nn_match_stacked(strategy, galleries::AbstractDict, features;\n                 backend, index = nothing, min_similarity = 0.6, chunk_bytes = 0)\n    -> Dict{K, Vector{NNMatch}}\n\nMatch features against every gallery in galleries with one stacked GEMM. Per gallery the result is identical to matching that gallery alone with strategy (MutualNN reverse checks are block-local; KnnMatch(k) takes any k): it is the matcher match runs, with the galleries as blocks of its rows. Matches use LOCAL indices: m.first indexes into gallery k's rows, m.second into features. Supply a prebuilt StackedDescriptorIndex as index to skip the per-call stack/upload.\n\nWhen index is given it — not galleries — defines what gets matched: the returned dictionary has one entry per index.keys, and galleries supplies only the key type K. The two key sets therefore need NOT agree, and callers rely on that: a caller narrowing its gallery set across calls can keep ONE index built over the full set rather than restaging descriptors every call, and simply ignore the extra entries. Build the index from the widest set you will ever match against.\n\nThe score matrix is built a row range at a time. chunk_bytes > 0 caps each range's score block (rows × features) at that many bytes; the default 0 sizes the ranges from free device memory. The result does not depend on the chunking. Scores are cosine similarities in the wider of the index's and the features' float type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.nn_match_stacked"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescribedFeature","page":"Matching","text":"DescribedFeature{D<:Detection, N}\n\nA feature detection paired with a fixed-length descriptor vector.\n\nWraps a Detection (frame + metadata) with an SVector{N, Float32} descriptor. All detection properties (.center, .σ, .frame, .metadata, etc.) are forwarded transparently via @generated getproperty.\n\nUse with StructArray for efficient columnar storage and zero-copy descriptor matrix extraction:\n\nsa = described_features(detections, desc_matrix)\nmatches = VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match(sa_board, sa_query, MutualNN())\nscored = csponds(sa_board, sa_query, matches)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescribedFeature"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.described_features-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection}, AbstractMatrix{Float32}}","page":"Matching","text":"described_features(detections, desc_matrix) -> StructArray{DescribedFeature}\n\nBuild a StructArray of DescribedFeature from a detection vector and an N×D descriptor matrix. The descriptor dimension D becomes the static size parameter of each SVector{D, Float32}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.described_features"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.descriptor_matrix-Tuple{StructArrays.StructArray{<:DescribedFeature}}","page":"Matching","text":"descriptor_matrix(sa::StructArray{<:DescribedFeature}) -> AbstractMatrix{Float32}\n\nZero-copy N×D descriptor matrix view from a StructArray of DescribedFeature. Suitable for supplying directly to match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.descriptor_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match-2","page":"Matching","text":"match(f1::StructArray{<:DescribedFeature}, f2::StructArray{<:DescribedFeature},\n      strategy; kwargs...) -> Vector{NNMatch}\n\nMatch described features by their descriptors. Extracts descriptor matrices and delegates to the matrix-based match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LOMA_IMAGE_SIZE","page":"Matching","text":"LOMA_IMAGE_SIZE = 784\n\nThe side of the DeDoDe descriptor graph's fixed square input: a LoMaDescriptor resamples an image of another size to (3, 784, 784).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LOMA_IMAGE_SIZE"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaDescriptor","page":"Matching","text":"LoMaDescriptor(variant = :b; max_detections = 2048, bf16 = false, execution_provider = :auto,\n               threads = Threads.nthreads())\n\nA DeDoDe descriptor of LoMa: :b (DeDoDe-B, 128-dimensional, 54 MB) or :g (DeDoDe-G, 256-dimensional, 1.3 GB; 690 MB with bf16 = true, which needs ONNX Runtime's CUDA provider). The model is downloaded on first use and cached. On the CUDA provider its session's arena is capped and the detector's plans leave it room (they are sized without the memory ONNX Runtime may hold), so neither releases the other. (descriptor)(img, detections) describes about max_detections of the strongest detections of img, spread over it (spread_strongest at its default tolerance), and returns a StructArray{DescribedFeature}.\n\nexecution_provider is ONNX Runtime's: :auto runs on CUDA when onnx_cuda_status finds a device and the CUDA 12 libraries (the environment holds CUDA_Runtime_jll and CUDNN_jll; ONNX Runtime's CUDA build, conda-forge's, is installed on first use), and on the CPU with a warning naming why otherwise; :cuda is an error where CUDA is unavailable, naming why; :cpu is the CPU. ONNX Runtime's intra-op pool has threads threads (the inter-op pool 1; no core pinning). loma_provenance records the provider used and the threads.\n\nThe network is DeDoDe's (Edstedt et al., 3DV 2024), as exported by LoMa; cite both (BibTeX in the package README).\n\nimg is a (3, H, W) RGB channelview in [0, 1] of any size and real element type, as Float32.(channelview(rgb)) gives it, and detections are in its COLMAP pixel frame. The exported graph's input is fixed at LOMA_IMAGE_SIZE² and feeds the first convolution directly, with no normalization: an image of another size is resampled to it per channel by VisualGeometryToolkitCore's bilinear image_warp! (the COLMAP scaling x ↦ (W, H) / 784 ⊙ x, no prefilter), and a (3, 784, 784) image is taken as it is. The keypoints enter the network's [-1, 1] chart from the image's own size, so the detections are never rescaled, and the described features carry them as given (frame in = frame out). Do not ImageNet-normalize the image: on LoMa's toronto pair that drops the matches from 166 to 14 (and none above probability 0.5).\n\nA descriptor pairs with the LoMaMatchers of its dimension: :b with :b128, :g with :b, :l, :g and :r.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaDescriptor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaMatcher","page":"Matching","text":"LoMaMatcher(variant = :b128; bf16 = false, execution_provider = :auto, threads = Threads.nthreads())\n\nA LoMa matcher: :b128 (48 MB) on 128-dimensional DeDoDe-B descriptors, or :b (48 MB), :l (184 MB), :g (726 MB) and :r (48 MB) on 256-dimensional DeDoDe-G ones (LoMaDescriptor(:g)). bf16 = true takes the half-size bf16 export of any of them, which needs ONNX Runtime's CUDA provider (its CPU provider lacks bf16 kernels). The model is downloaded on first use and cached; see the call method for matching. execution_provider (:auto, :cpu or :cuda) and threads are LoMaDescriptor's; loma_provenance records them.\n\nThe network is LoMa's (Nordström et al., \"LoMa: Local Feature Matching Revisited\", ECCV 2026); cite it (BibTeX in the package README).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaMatcher"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaMatcher-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Matching","text":"(matcher::LoMaMatcher)(features_a, features_b, size_a, size_b; min_score = 0) -> Vector{NNMatch}\n\nMatch two sets of LoMaDescriptor features, whose descriptor dimension must be the matcher's (ArgumentError otherwise). size_a/size_b are the image sizes, (H, W) or a channelview's (C, H, W).\n\nReturns the same type as match: m.first indexes features_a, m.second indexes features_b, sorted best-first. m.score is the network's match probability (higher is better, a similarity, so the default csponds(features_a, features_b, matches) reads it correctly); m.ratio is Inf32, since the network has no second neighbour. Matches scoring below min_score are dropped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaMatcher"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.loma_provenance-Tuple{Any}","page":"Matching","text":"loma_provenance(l::Union{LoMaDescriptor, LoMaMatcher}) -> NamedTuple\n\nWhat runs l, as plain data for a result to record: model (the release file) and its sha256, the execution_provider actually used (:cpu or :cuda, after :auto is resolved), the ONNX Runtime intra-op threads, the onnxruntime version and, on CUDA, the cuda_driver, cuda_runtime and cudnn versions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.loma_provenance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.OnnxCudaStatus","page":"Matching","text":"OnnxCudaStatus\n\nWhether ONNX Runtime's CUDA provider can run in this process, probed once: available, and reason (empty when available) naming what is missing. With the provider available, driver (the CUDA driver API version), cuda_runtime and cudnn (the versions of the libraries loaded for it) are set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.OnnxCudaStatus"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.onnx_cuda_status-Tuple{}","page":"Matching","text":"onnx_cuda_status() -> OnnxCudaStatus\n\nONNX Runtime's CUDA provider in this process: probed (and its libraries loaded) on the first call, the same answer after. :auto runs on CUDA exactly when onnx_cuda_status().available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.onnx_cuda_status"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.onnx_session-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Matching","text":"onnx_session(path; execution_provider = :auto, threads = Threads.nthreads(), gpu_mem_limit = 8 * 2^30)\n    -> ONNXRunTime.InferenceSession\n\nAn ONNX Runtime session of the model file path, built as this package builds its own networks' (LoMa's): ONNXRunTime's load_inference with the session options it does not take. execution_provider is resolved by resolve_execution_provider (:auto runs on CUDA when onnx_cuda_status finds a device, on the CPU with a warning naming why otherwise; :cuda is an error where CUDA is unavailable; :cpu is the CPU), so a CUDA session runs ONNX Runtime's CUDA build this package installs, which has device code for every device from sm70 to sm120, the B200's sm100 among them (Microsoft's GPU builds lack sm100). The session's execution_provider field holds the resolved provider.\n\nONNX Runtime's intra-op pool has threads threads (its core pinning off), its inter-op pool one. On CUDA, the session's device arena is capped at gpu_mem_limit bytes and grows by what each run requests; a run that needs more is ONNX Runtime's out-of-memory error. The ASIFT detector's device plans leave free the room the process's live CUDA sessions may still take (each cap less what its arena holds, measured around the package's own runs; a session released or collected reserves nothing), so a session and the detector share the device without either releasing the other's memory. The plans cached before a CUDA session is created are released once, then. On the CPU gpu_mem_limit is ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.onnx_session"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.resolve_execution_provider","page":"Matching","text":"resolve_execution_provider(execution_provider) -> Symbol\n\nThe ONNX Runtime provider execution_provider runs on here: :auto is :cuda when onnx_cuda_status finds the CUDA provider available, and :cpu with a warning naming why otherwise; :cuda is :cuda, or an error naming why it is unavailable; :cpu is :cpu. Anything else is an ArgumentError. What LoMaDescriptor and LoMaMatcher run on; a caller that keys a cache on the provider resolves it first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.resolve_execution_provider"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Detection","page":"Detection","text":"","title":"Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#The-ASIFT-detector","page":"Detection","text":"asift_detect is the package's detector. It implements IMAS-25, the affine-invariant SIFT of the IMAS family. The image is resampled under 25 simulated camera tilts (the identity, 8 tilts of 2.62 and 16 tilts of 5.18 at evenly spaced longitudes). DoG extrema are found in each warp, refined to sub-pixel accuracy, mapped back to the input image as elliptic frames, and merged across warps by a non-maximum suppression. The whole pipeline runs as KernelAbstractions kernels, so the same code runs on the CPU (the default) and on any GPU backend (device = CUDABackend(), for instance).\n\nThe tilt schedule is a keyword, a tilt_schedule name or a vector of (t, φ) pairs. tilts = :untilted, the identity alone, is plain DoG-SIFT. Its keypoints localize best (about 0.3 px rms on real panorama frames, against about 0.95 px for a tilted warp's), so it is the schedule for views that differ by a rotation, such as a panorama's:\n\ndets = asift_detect(img)                       # IMAS-25 (tilts = :imas25)\nsift = asift_detect(img; tilts = :untilted)    # DoG-SIFT: the identity warp alone\n\nexclusion_mask, an H×W Bool matrix, marks the pixels where detection is not allowed (clipped highlights, a vignetted rim): a detection whose centre lies within three of its scales of a masked pixel is dropped. The detector on a variance-stabilized RAW (variance_stabilize) masks the sensor's saturated pixels by the same rule.\n\nclipped = Float32.(Gray.(img)) .>= 0.99f0\ndets = asift_detect(img; exclusion_mask = clipped)\n\nThe other keywords choose the non-maximum suppression (nms = :mxm, :ellipse or :none), gate by polarity (polarity = :dark or :light), restrict suppression to features of similar scale (band_octaves), and set the detection thresholds. The asift_detect docstring lists them.\n\nThe result does not depend on the threads or the device's scheduling: the same image gives the same detections, in the same order, run to run.","title":"The ASIFT detector"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Plans-and-their-cache","page":"Detection","text":"The detector's buffers for one image size, device and tilt schedule are an AsiftPlan. asift_detect builds plans on demand and keeps the most recent one: a plan for a large image under the full schedule holds several GiB of device memory. Process frames grouped by resolution to reuse it. set_max_cached_plans! keeps more plans, and clear_asift_plans! releases them. To manage a plan yourself, build it and call asift_detect!, which returns the raw ImasFeatureKA records; asift_detections converts them.","title":"Plans and their cache"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Pixel-origin","page":"Detection","text":"Every detector of the package returns COLMAP pixel centres. The top-left pixel's centre is (0.5, 0.5), so the pixel img[r, c] has its centre at (c − 0.5, r − 0.5). A center is (x, y): x along the image columns and y along the rows.\n\nasift_detect(img; raw = true) returns the raw features instead, in 0-indexed OpenCV coordinates (top-left pixel centre at (0, 0)), which are 0.5 less on both axes.\n\nThe package follows the stack's rule that a function returns results in the frame its inputs came in: pixels in, pixels out. The matchers work in descriptor space and never touch coordinates, so nothing here normalizes.","title":"Pixel origin"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Feature-frames-are-shapes","page":"Detection","text":"A Detection composes a feature frame with the detector's metadata. \"Frame\" is meant in the VLFeat and Kornia sense: the shape of a detected feature, not a coordinate system. The frames are VisualGeometryToolkitCore types, ordered by degrees of freedom:\n\nFrame DOF Shape\nDisc 3 centre and isotropic scale\nOrientedDisc 4 centre, scale and orientation\nEllipticFrame 5 centre and shape matrix (anisotropic)\nAffineFrame 6 centre and full affine shape\n\nasift_detect returns Detection{EllipticFrame}. The accessor is feature_frame(d). Properties of the frame and of the metadata read directly: d.center, d.σ (the characteristic scale), d.Σ (the shape matrix), d.response.\n\nCoordinate-frame tags, such as ImageFrame and NormalizedImageFrame, are a separate vocabulary that lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCore. The two share the word \"frame\" and nothing else.","title":"Feature frames are shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Polarity","page":"Detection","text":"ASIFT records the sign of the DoG response in the metadata field laplacian_scale_score: positive for a dark blob on a lighter background and negative for a light one. is_dark and is_light read it. Detections whose metadata has no such field are neither; the trait polarity_trait tells the two kinds of metadata apart.","title":"Polarity"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Accuracy-of-the-sub-pixel-refinement","page":"Detection","text":"The quadratic sub-pixel refinement pulls a centre toward the nearest pixel centre. On noise-free isotropic Gaussian blobs on the CPU, measured by the test suite:\n\na blob centred on a pixel centre comes back exact, within 0.005 px;\nbetween pixel centres the pull is at most 0.03 px at σ = 4, and at most 0.06 px for σ from 2 to 8.\n\nLarger blobs and clutter do worse. The Monte Carlo study in the package's benchmark/mc directory measured a centre bias of 0.1 to 0.2 px on clean blobs with σ ≥ 6.5, and up to about 1 px in clutter. Treat 0.06 px as the bound for small, isolated blobs only.","title":"Accuracy of the sub-pixel refinement"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Keypoint-uncertainty","page":"Detection","text":"asift_detect(img; uncertainty = FeatureCovariance()) returns each detection with its frame as VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Uncertain(frame, Σ̃). cov(d) is a 3×3 covariance over (x, y, log σ) in the image frame, for unit iid pixel noise in the detector's intensity units (the [0, 1] scale for 8-bit input). The physical covariance is σ_n²·Σ̃ for a noise standard deviation σ_n. Consumers that need only the position take the top-left 2×2 block.\n\nThe covariance follows from the implicit function theorem at the refined DoG extremum; its noise term is tabulated exactly for the discrete pyramid once per plan. The FeatureCovariance docstring states the model. The Monte Carlo study that calibrates it is benchmark/mc/keypoint_uncertainty_mc.jl, and benchmark/mc/zeisl_comparison.jl compares it with the covariance of Zeisl et al. (BMVC 2009). The default, uncertainty = nothing, compiles the detector exactly as without the option.","title":"Keypoint uncertainty"},{"category":"section","location":"api/detection/#Detection","page":"Detection","text":"The detection type and its polarity helpers, the ASIFT detector and its plans, the keypoint covariance, the MSER and Förstner detectors, and the spatially spread selection of detections (SSC). The image-input helpers and the PNG and RAW readers are on Image Input.","title":"Detection"},{"category":"module","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures","page":"Detection","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\nFeature detection and descriptor matching.\n\nDetection: ASIFT (IMAS-25) on KernelAbstractions. asift_detect runs on the CPU or any KernelAbstractions GPU backend and returns Detections (a frame plus metadata). VisualGeometryToolkitIO writes and reads them as JSON (json_value, from_json).\nMatching: nearest-neighbour descriptor matching, one KernelAbstractions implementation on the CPU or any GPU backend (nn_match, match), and a stacked matcher over many galleries (nn_match_stacked).\nLoMa: the learned DeDoDe descriptors and LoMa matchers, from the authors' ONNX exports (LoMaDescriptor, LoMaMatcher).\nCamera RAW: the stack's one RAW path. load_raw reads a Bayer RAW file as exact linear values on the 2×2 Bayer cells (the green-cell image for geometry and radiometry, or R G B), with saturation flags, true sample positions and the exact frame map onto the raw (raw_frame_map); a display-only demosaic for figures.\nExtensions: JLD2 backs FileCache; Makie draws detections and correspondences; LibRaw_jll is the RAW decoder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Disc-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Disc}}","page":"Detection","text":"Disc(d::Detection) -> Disc\n\nExtract or convert to a Disc frame (uses characteristic_scale for anisotropic).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Disc"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FeatureFrame}}","page":"Detection","text":"Ellipse(d::Detection; cutoff=1.0) -> Ellipse\n\nConvert to an ellipse at the cutoff·σ contour (via region).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.EllipticFrame-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.EllipticFrame}}","page":"Detection","text":"EllipticFrame(d::Detection) -> EllipticFrame\n\nExtract or convert to an EllipticFrame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.EllipticFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.Detection","page":"Detection","text":"Detection{F<:FeatureFrame, M}\n\nType alias for Attributed{F, M} — a detection result composing a geometric frame with detector-specific metadata.\n\nThe frame type F determines the geometry:\n\nDetection{Disc{T}, M} - isotropic detection (3 DOF)\nDetection{OrientedDisc{T}, M} - oriented isotropic detection (4 DOF)\nDetection{EllipticFrame{T}, M} - anisotropic detection (5 DOF)\nDetection{AffineFrame{T}, M} - full affine detection (6 DOF)\nDetection{<:Uncertain{F}, M} - a frame F with its covariance (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Uncertain(frame, Σ)), from asift_detect(…; uncertainty = FeatureCovariance()): cov(d) is the covariance, feature_frame(d) the bare frame, geom(d) the detection without it, and the properties below read as on the bare detection\n\nType Parameters\n\nF<:FeatureFrame: The geometric frame type\nM: Metadata, a NamedTuple. ASIFT emits (response, edge_score, laplacian_scale_score, warp_idx); laplacian_scale_score is the polarity field is_dark/is_light read (> 0 dark, < 0 light).\n\nFields (from Attributed)\n\nvalue::F: The geometric frame (center + shape)\nattributes::M: Detector-specific scores and information\n\nCoordinates\n\nThe frame's center is (x, y) in COLMAP pixel-centre coordinates, the convention of every detector in this package: x along the image columns, y along the rows, the top-left pixel's centre at (0.5, 0.5). The pixel img[r, c] has its centre at (c − 0.5, r − 0.5). asift_detect documents its measured sub-pixel accuracy.\n\nProperty Access\n\nProperties are forwarded from frame and metadata:\n\nd.frame, d.value → the geometric frame\nd.metadata, d.attributes → the metadata\nd.center → frame.center\nd.σ → scale from frame (exact for Disc, characteristic_scale for anisotropic)\nd.Σ → shape matrix (blob shape, not measurement covariance)\nd.response, d.edge_score, etc. → forwarded from metadata\n\nExample\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> det = Detection(Disc(Point2(100.0, 200.0), 5.0), (response = 0.05, laplacian_scale_score = -0.1));\n\njulia> Tuple(det.center), det.σ, det.response   # from the frame and the metadata\n((100.0, 200.0), 5.0, 0.05)\n\njulia> is_light(det)\ntrue\n\nSee also: Disc, EllipticFrame, VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Attributed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.Detection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.HasPolarity","page":"Detection","text":"HasPolarity()\n\nThe HasPolarityTrait of detection metadata that records a polarity: a NamedTuple with a laplacian_scale_score field, positive for a dark blob and negative for a light one. is_dark and is_light read that field's sign. Public, not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.HasPolarity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.HasPolarityTrait","page":"Detection","text":"HasPolarityTrait\n\nHoly trait for metadata types indicating whether they contain polarity information: HasPolarity or NoPolarity, as polarity_trait returns it. Public, not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.HasPolarityTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NoPolarity","page":"Detection","text":"NoPolarity()\n\nThe HasPolarityTrait of detection metadata that records no polarity. is_dark and is_light are both false for such a detection. Public, not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NoPolarity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate-Tuple{Any, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Attributed{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FeatureFrame, <:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.CovMat}, <:NamedTuple}}","page":"Detection","text":"propagate(m, d::Detection{<:Uncertain}) -> Uncertain\n\nThe uncertain centre of a detection through the map m (a frame map onto a camera's pixels, say): its centre with the position block — the top-left 2 × 2, (x, y) — of its frame's (x, y, log σ) covariance (FeatureCovariance), carried through m by VisualGeometryToolkitCore's propagate(m, ::Uncertain).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.propagate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.is_dark-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection}","page":"Detection","text":"is_dark(d::Detection) -> Bool\n\nWhether d is a dark blob on a lighter background: its metadata records a polarity (see polarity_trait) and its laplacian_scale_score is positive. false for a detection without a recorded polarity.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> is_dark(Detection(Disc(Point2(1.0, 2.0), 3.0), 0.5; polarity = :dark))\ntrue\n\njulia> is_dark(Detection(Disc(Point2(1.0, 2.0), 3.0), 0.5))   # no polarity recorded\nfalse\n\nSee also is_light.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.is_dark"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.is_light-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection}","page":"Detection","text":"is_light(d::Detection) -> Bool\n\nWhether d is a light blob on a darker background: its metadata records a polarity (see polarity_trait) and its laplacian_scale_score is negative. false for a detection without a recorded polarity. ASIFT detections record one, from the sign of the DoG response.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> d = Detection(Disc(Point2(1.0, 2.0), 3.0), 0.5; polarity = :light);\n\njulia> is_light(d), is_dark(d)\n(true, false)\n\nSee also is_dark.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.is_light"},{"category":"function","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.polarity_trait","page":"Detection","text":"polarity_trait(::Type{M}) -> HasPolarityTrait\npolarity_trait(d::Detection) -> HasPolarityTrait\n\nWhether metadata of type M, or the metadata of the detection d, records a polarity: HasPolarity for a NamedTuple with a laplacian_scale_score field, NoPolarity otherwise. A detector whose metadata is another type opts in by adding a method for its type, and must then give it a laplacian_scale_score property. Public, not exported.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> import VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures as VgtkFeatures\n\njulia> VgtkFeatures.polarity_trait(Detection(Disc(Point2(1.0, 2.0), 3.0), (response = 1.0,)))\nVisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NoPolarity()\n\njulia> VgtkFeatures.polarity_trait(typeof((response = 1.0, laplacian_scale_score = -1.0)))\nVisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.HasPolarity()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.polarity_trait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.MserRegion","page":"Detection","text":"MserRegion\n\nOne maximally stable extremal region, as detect_mser returns it.\n\nFields\n\nseed::CartesianIndex{2}: the region's reference pixel, a pixel at the region's level (the tree node MaxTree chose; VLFeat may pick another pixel of the same plateau). A VLFeat seed s (0-based linear index into the column-major H × W image given with dims = (H, W)) is the pixel CartesianIndices(img)[s + 1], and ours is VLFeat's index LinearIndices(img)[seed] - 1.\nlevel::UInt8: the region's level in the input image: a :dark region is the 8-connected component of img .<= level holding seed, a :light one that of img .>= level. img[seed] == level.\narea::Int: its number of pixels.\nvariation::Float32: VLFeat's (in)stability (|R₊Δ| − |R|) / |R|.\npolarity::Symbol: :dark or :light.\n\nThe seed and level determine the region, so its pixels and boundary are not stored (nested regions would hold every pixel many times over): they are formed on demand from the image the region was detected in — region_mask(region, img) the pixels as a mask (VLFeat's vl_erfill), boundary_contours(region, img) their boundary as closed polygons. is_dark and is_light read the polarity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.MserRegion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.boundary_contours-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Bool}}","page":"Detection","text":"boundary_contours(pixels) -> Vector{Vector{Point2{Float64}}}\nboundary_contours(region::MserRegion, img) -> Vector{Vector{Point2{Float64}}}\n\nThe boundary of a set of pixels (CartesianIndex{2} (row, column), a Bool mask, or an MSER of img — its region_mask), traced along the pixel edges: one closed polygon per boundary curve (the last vertex joins the first), vertices only where the boundary turns, with x along the columns and y along the rows in the COLMAP pixel origin (conventions/pixel-origins.md), so the pixel (r, c) is the square [c − 1, c] × [r − 1, r] and every vertex is a pixel corner with integer coordinates. The polygons are sorted by signed shoelace area, descending: the outer boundaries first (positive, clockwise on screen), the largest first, then the holes (negative), the smallest first and the largest last. Diagonal neighbours are connected (8-connectivity, the connectivity of an MSER), so where two pixels of the set meet at a corner one curve runs through it; holes are 4-connected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.boundary_contours"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detect_mser-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Detection","text":"detect_mser(img; delta = 5, min_area = 3 / length(img), max_area = 0.75,\n            max_variation = 0.25, min_diversity = 0.2,\n            bright_on_dark = true, dark_on_bright = true) -> Vector{MserRegion}\n\nThe maximally stable extremal regions (MSER; Matas, Chum, Urban and Pajdla, \"Robust wide baseline stereo from maximally stable extremal regions\", BMVC\n\nof the 8-bit gray image img, selected as VLFeat's vl_mser selects\n\nthem (Andrea Vedaldi and Brian Fulkerson, BSD license; src/mser.jl carries the notice). VLFeat's stability criterion is its own, simpler than the paper's.\n\nThe extremal regions are the 8-connected components of the level sets img .<= l, arranged in a tree by ImageMorphology's MaxTree. The variation of a region R at level l is (|R₊Δ| − |R|) / |R|, where R₊Δ is the largest region containing R at a level at most l + delta. Of a region and its parent region exactly one level up, the one of larger variation (on a tie, the child) is not maximally stable; the root never is. The survivors are then filtered, largest first:\n\nmax_variation: a region of variation >= max_variation is dropped;\nmax_area, min_area: FRACTIONS of length(img); a region larger than max_area * length(img) or smaller than min_area * length(img) pixels is dropped (the default min_area is three pixels);\nmin_diversity: a region whose area differs from that of its nearest surviving enclosing region by less than min_diversity times the latter's area is a duplicate and is dropped (min_diversity = 1 turns this off).\n\nAn image of fewer than three pixels has no region (VLFeat returns none): MserRegion[], the area fractions not checked.\n\ndark_on_bright returns the dark regions (polarity = :dark) and bright_on_dark the light ones (:light), the components of img .>= l: VLFeat runs the same on 255 .- img, whose <= level sets these are. Dark regions come first; each polarity is ordered as VLFeat orders it, by level (darkest first, resp. brightest first), then by seed.\n\nInput\n\nA matrix of UInt8, N0f8 or Gray{N0f8}: VLFeat's vl_mser_pix is a byte, and these hold its 256 levels exactly. Any other element type is an ArgumentError: quantize explicitly (e.g. gray_u8), so the levels the regions are cut at are the caller's choice.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures: boundary_contours\n\njulia> img = fill(0xc8, 40, 60); img[11:20, 21:35] .= 0x28;   # a dark rectangle\n\njulia> r = only(detect_mser(img; bright_on_dark = false));\n\njulia> r.polarity, r.area, Tuple.(only(boundary_contours(r, img)))\n(:dark, 150, [(20.0, 10.0), (35.0, 10.0), (35.0, 20.0), (20.0, 20.0)])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detect_mser"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.region_mask-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.MserRegion, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Detection","text":"region_mask(region::MserRegion, img) -> BitMatrix\n\nThe pixels of region, an MSER of img, as a mask the size of img: the 8-connected component of img .<= region.level (:dark; .>= for :light) holding region.seed, filled from the seed as VLFeat's vl_erfill fills it. findall of it lists the pixels in ascending linear order. img must be the image the region was detected in: a seed outside it, a level other than img[seed] or a fill of another area than region.area is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.region_mask"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detect_foerstner-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Detection","text":"detect_foerstner([T = Float32], img; device = default_device(),\n                 method = :foerstner, noise_sigma = 2.0,\n                 derivative = :gaussian2d, integration = :box,\n                 derivative_sigma = 1.0, integration_sigma = 1.41 * derivative_sigma,\n                 precision_threshold = 0.5, roundness_threshold = 0.3,\n                 significance_level = 0.999) -> StructArray{<:Detection}\n\nThe Förstner interest operator (Förstner and Gülch, 1987): the junction (corner) points and circular points of a grey image at sub-pixel precision, each with its covariance, after the reference implementation ip_fop.m (Luxen, 2005), with Köthe's doubled resolution and window criterion (Köthe, 2003).\n\nReturns one row per optimal window, a StructArray of Detection(Uncertain(PointFrame(x), Σ), (; label, window, F)) (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's PointFrame: a position, no scale):\n\nlabel: :corner (a junction point), :circular (Förstner & Gülch's circular point: the centre of a rotationally symmetric pattern, with no size estimate) or :unknown (the significance test assigned neither);\nthe frame: the corner or circular point's position and its 2×2 covariance (px², (x, y) order); for an :unknown row the window centre with a NaN covariance (the reference returns no estimate for it);\nwindow: the window centre, a Point2;\nF: the classification statistic Ω_corner / Ω_circular.\n\nfilter(d -> d.label === :corner, points) are the corners; Ellipse(d; sigma = 1) is a point's 1σ error ellipse (ip_errell.m's default).\n\nimg is any grey image the package's detectors take, read through gray_f32 as grey values in [0, 1] (an 8-bit image as value/255, as MATLAB's im2double); it is not stretched to its own range. The image-wide steps run on device, any KernelAbstractions backend, in precision T (the reference computes in Float64; supply Float64 for that).\n\nKeywords (defaults: the values the reference's CODE runs with)\n\nmethod: :foerstner — windows where the roundness 4 det/tr² > roundness_threshold and the precision det/tr > (σₙ / precision_threshold)², ranked by det/tr; :koethe — windows whose precision test succeeds, ranked by the smaller eigenvalue.\nnoise_sigma: the standard deviation of the (constant) image noise, in GREY LEVELS OF AN 8-BIT IMAGE (0–255), as the reference's for a uint8 image; σₙ = noise_sigma / 255 on the [0, 1] image.\nderivative: :gaussian2d (a Gaussian derivative smoothed across by a Gaussian of the same σ) or :gaussian1d (the derivative alone).\nintegration: :box (a box whose standard deviation is integration_sigma) or :gaussian.\nderivative_sigma, integration_sigma: in input pixels.\nprecision_threshold: the largest accepted point standard deviation, input px.\nroundness_threshold: the smallest accepted roundness (:foerstner only).\nsignificance_level: of the F-test that labels a point corner or circular.\n\nAlgorithm (ip_fop.m, step by step)\n\nThe image is doubled bilinearly (the package's one 2× upsampling, ASIFT's) and every scale and threshold doubled with it (lines 170–180).\nThe gradient by correlation with the derivative taps (radius ⌈3.3σ⌉, zero padding, as imfilter), its squares and product (the squared gradient), and their integration by the box (width 2⌈√(12σ² + 1)/2⌉ + 1) or the Gaussian (radius ⌈3.3σ⌉), zero padded (lines 184–207, 617–678).\nThe window measure (det/tr or the smaller eigenvalue) masked by the tests above; its regional maxima (8-connected plateaus higher than every neighbour, imregionalmax), thinned by a second non-maximum suppression over a square of radius 2⌈√(12σᵢ² + 1)⌉ doubled pixels, away from the border (lines 210–281).\nIn the (2ρ + 1)² window around each centre, ρ = ⌈√(12σᵢ² + 1)/2⌉ + 1 (σᵢ doubled), the pixels weighed equally (the reference's choice, also for a Gaussian integration): the corner is the least-squares intersection of the lines through every pixel ACROSS its gradient (normal matrix Σ ∇g∇gᵀ), the circular point that of the lines ALONG it (Σ ∇g⊥∇g⊥ᵀ). Each has a weighted residual sum Ω, redundancy (2ρ + 1)² − 2 and covariance Ω/redundancy · N⁻¹ (lines 314–423, 440, 449).\nF = Ω_corner / Ω_circular against the F distribution of (redundancy, redundancy) degrees of freedom, as the reference's (T < F(α)) − (T > F(1 − α)) (line 429): a corner below the 1 − significance_level quantile, a circular point above the significance_level quantile, unknown in between.\n\nCoordinates\n\nCOLMAP pixel centres of the input image, (x, y) = (column, row), converted once, at the output. The doubled image's pixel (r′, c′) (1-based) samples the input at 1-based (r′/2, c′/2), so a doubled-grid position is at COLMAP ((c′ − 1)/2, (r′ − 1)/2) in the input, and its covariance is the doubled one /4, reordered to (x, y).\n\nDifferences from ip_fop.m\n\nUndoing the doubling — fixed. The reference doubles with MATLAB's imresize(g, 2, 'bilinear') (line 172; doubled pixel j samples input j/2 + 1/4) but maps positions back with transf_H, transf_v (lines 340–350, applied at 436, 446, 457), the inverse of an align-corners grid: its points are off by ((n + 1)/2 − x)/(2n − 1) (1-based, n the image size), 0 at the centre, up to 0.25 px toward the centre at the borders. Here the doubling is the package's upsampling (input j/2, a quarter pixel from imresize's grid) undone by its own exact inverse.\nThe circular point's residual sum — kept as the reference has it. Its line 423 takes two of the three terms from the corner model's residuals; the circular model's own residual sum would take all three from its own. Monte Carlo coverage favours the reference's form, so it is kept.\nDefaults — the code's, not the header's. The header states SIGMA_N = 2.5 and SIGMA_DIFF = 0.7 (lines 37, 50); the code runs 2.0 and 1.0 (lines 521, 524). A documentation conflict, not a fix.\n\nThe docs page gives the evidence for each. The reference warns that with a Gaussian integration the window estimation still weighs the pixels equally; so does this.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> img = [Float32((c - 20.5)^2 + (r - 24.5)^2 < 6.25) for r in 1:48, c in 1:40];   # a dot at pixel (24.5, 20.5)\n\njulia> points = detect_foerstner(img);\n\njulia> only(points).label\n:circular\n\njulia> round.(Tuple(only(points).center); digits = 1)   # COLMAP: (column − ½, row − ½)\n(20.0, 24.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detect_foerstner"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.batched_rows-Tuple{Any, Int64, Int64}","page":"Detection","text":"batched_rows(run_chunk, N::Int, max_batch::Int; retry_single = false) -> Matrix{Float32}\n\nRun run_chunk(offset, n) over 1:N in chunks of at most max_batch rows (max_batch <= 0 means one chunk) and assemble the N × D result: the row width D is read off the FIRST chunk's output (any shape with n * D elements), the matrix is allocated lazily then, and each chunk is assigned into its contiguous row slice. With retry_single, a chunk that throws is retried one row at a time (the batch size drops to 1 for the rest of the run) — the recovery an inference session wants when a batch overflows its arena. N == 0 returns an empty 0 × 0 matrix.\n\nHoisted from BlobBoards, where the ONNX host, ORT device-tensor and two Lux describe backends had each drifted their own copy (2026-09).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.batched_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detect-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Detection","text":"asift_detect(img; device=default_device(), nms=:mxm, window_half=3,\n             cutoff=3.0, intensity_thr=0.04, edge_thr=10.0,\n             polarity=:none, band_octaves=0.0, band_reach=1, boundary_K=3.0,\n             tilts=:imas25, exclusion_mask=nothing, upsample=true, seed_sigma=1.6,\n             raw=false, uncertainty=nothing)\n\nRun the IMAS-25 ASIFT detector on img (H×W; Gray, UInt8, or real-valued in [0,1]) and return Vector{Detection{EllipticFrame}} in COLMAP coords.\n\nCoordinates. A detection's center is (x, y), with x along the image columns and y along the rows, in COLMAP pixel-centre coordinates: the top-left pixel's centre is (0.5, 0.5), so a blob centred on the pixel img[r, c] is returned at (c − 0.5, r − 0.5). Measured on isotropic Gaussian blobs on the CPU (pinned by the test suite):\n\na blob centred on a pixel centre comes back exact (within 0.005 px);\nbetween pixel centres, the quadratic sub-pixel refinement pulls the centre toward the nearest pixel centre, by at most 0.03 px at σ = 4 (−0.007, −0.015, −0.021, −0.027 px at fractions 0.1–0.4, mirrored above 0.5) and 0.06 px for σ from 2 to 8;\nan exact half-pixel centre, where the DoG ties between the two pixels, is refined like any other: the extremum test breaks the tie toward the first pixel, so it carries that side's bias (−0.031 px at σ = 4). A test strict against all 26 neighbours would reject both pixels;\nevery detection is refined: extrema are searched on the interior scale slices only (each with a slice above and below), and an extremum whose refinement fails is dropped (plan.refine_dropped counts them), never returned on the octave's pixel grid; with uncertainty = FeatureCovariance(), so is a detection whose covariance is singular or NaN (counted there too).\n\nWith raw = true the features are in 0-indexed OpenCV coordinates (top-left pixel centre (0, 0)), 0.5 less on both axes.\n\nnms selects the cross-warp non-max suppression: :mxm (centroid window, default), :ellipse (Mahalanobis footprint), or :none (full per-warp feature pool). polarity gates by DoG sign (:dark → response > 0, :light → response < 0, :none → both) BEFORE the cross-warp NMS, so a blob of the other sign never suppresses a kept one. band_octaves > 0 makes the NMS scale-banded: a feature can only be suppressed by features whose geometric-mean scale band ⌊log2(√det S) / band_octaves⌋ is within band_reach of its own, so board-sized extrema and tilted-warp streaks stop erasing the small blobs inside them (see asift_detect!). raw=true returns the ImasFeatureKA vector instead of Detections. device, tilts, upsample and seed_sigma pick the plan; the others but raw and exclusion_mask go to asift_detect!, which refuses a keyword it does not take.\n\nTilts. tilts is a tilt_schedule name or a vector of (t, φ) pairs: :imas25 (the default) the 25 tilts of IMAS-25, :untilted the identity warp alone (plain DoG-SIFT), whose keypoints localize best (about 0.3 px rms on real panorama frames, against about 0.95 px for a tilted warp's): the schedule for views that differ by a rotation, such as a panorama's. Under :untilted every detection has warp_idx == 1, and they are exactly the identity warp's detections under :imas25.\n\nExclusion mask. exclusion_mask, an H×W AbstractMatrix{Bool} (true where detection is not allowed: clipped or saturated pixels, a vignetted rim, an overlay), drops every detection whose centre lies within three of its scales (characteristic_scale, the DoG's support) of a masked pixel, so no keypoint is read off masked pixels. It applies after the cross-warp NMS, to raw features and covariances alike. The detector on a StabilizedImage masks its saturated pixels by this rule.\n\nDeterminism. The result is a function of the image and the keywords: the same keypoints, in the same order, run to run, on one thread or many, launched statically or from inside a threaded region. The extrema pool fills in the order the threads reach it, so before the cross-warp NMS (which breaks ties in its quantized response by pool order) it is sorted into a canonical order: by position, scale and response, then every other field (the covariance's too). The detections come back in that order.\n\nKeypoint uncertainty. uncertainty = FeatureCovariance() returns each detection's frame as Uncertain(frame, Σ̃), Σ̃ = cov(d) a 3×3 CovMat over (x, y, log σ) for unit iid pixel noise in the detector's intensity units — see FeatureCovariance for the model, the parameterization and the units. With raw = true the result is then (; features, cov). The default nothing runs the detector without it, the kernels compiled exactly as before.\n\nThe plan for (size, device, tilts, upsample, seed_sigma) is cached (see set_max_cached_plans!) and every call holds a process-wide lock, so concurrent calls are safe but do not run in parallel. On a KA.CPU() device every kernel launch is statically scheduled over the threads, or dynamically when the call runs inside a threaded region (e.g. a Threads.@threads loop over frames); the features are the same either way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detections-Tuple{Vector{ImasFeatureKA}}","page":"Detection","text":"asift_detections(feats::Vector{ImasFeatureKA}) -> Vector{Detection}\nasift_detections(feats, cov) -> Vector{Detection}\n\nConvert raw ASIFT features (0-indexed opencv pixel coords, input frame) into Detection{EllipticFrame}s in COLMAP convention (+0.5 on both axes, so the top-left pixel's centre is (0.5, 0.5); see asift_detect). The metadata is (response, edge_score, laplacian_scale_score, warp_idx): laplacian_scale_score = sign(response) (+1 dark, −1 light — the DoG sign convention polarity gates on), which is what is_dark and is_light read; edge_score is a placeholder (always 0). Given the features' covariances (asift_detect!(…; uncertainty = FeatureCovariance())), each frame is Uncertain(frame, Σ̃) (see FeatureCovariance); the +0.5 shift leaves a covariance unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detections"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.clear_asift_plans!-Tuple{}","page":"Detection","text":"clear_asift_plans!()\n\nDrop all cached AsiftPlans, releasing their device buffers immediately rather than leaving them to a finalizer.\n\nThe cache is keyed by (W, H, device, tilts, upsample, seed_sigma) and holds at most set_max_cached_plans! plans (default 1), evicting the least recently used. Call this to release the resident plans outright — e.g. between sequences in a mixed-resolution corpus, or before handing the device to another workload. The freed memory is returned to the device (on CUDA, CUDA.jl's pool is reclaimed), so an allocator outside the backend's pool, such as ONNX Runtime's, can use it: a LoMaDescriptor or LoMaMatcher on ONNX Runtime's CUDA provider calls this before it runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.clear_asift_plans!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.set_max_cached_plans!-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Detection","text":"set_max_cached_plans!(n) -> Int\n\nSet how many AsiftPlans stay resident (default 1). Evicted plans have their device buffers released immediately via VisualGeometryToolkitCore's free_device_buffers!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.set_max_cached_plans!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.AsiftPlan","page":"Detection","text":"AsiftPlan(W, H; device = default_device(), tilts = :imas25, seed_sigma = 1.6,\n          input_blur = 0.5, nb_scales = 3, capacity = 2^20, chunk = 0,\n          upsample = true)\n\nThe preallocated buffers of the ASIFT detector for W×H images on device (any KernelAbstractions backend): the tilted warps, the scale-space pyramids, the feature pool and the compiled kernels. asift_detect! runs the detector in them without allocating on the device.\n\nasift_detect builds and caches plans itself (see set_max_cached_plans! and clear_asift_plans!), so most callers never construct one. Build one to own its lifetime, or to detect in a loop over same-size frames without the cache's lock.\n\ntilts: the tilt schedule, a tilt_schedule name (:imas25, the default, or :untilted, the identity alone: plain DoG-SIFT) or a vector of (t, φ) pairs (tilt and longitude in radians).\nseed_sigma, input_blur, nb_scales, upsample: the SIFT pyramid's seed blur, the input's assumed blur, the scales per octave and whether the first octave is the 2× upsampled image.\ncapacity: the initial size of the feature pool; it grows when a frame produces more extrema.\nchunk: how many warps stay resident at once; 0 sizes the chunk from the device's free memory.\n\nA plan holds device memory (several GiB for a large image under the full schedule). It is mutable because its feature pool grows, and because plan.refine_dropped records how many extrema the last call dropped when their sub-pixel refinement failed (no unrefined detection is returned) or, with uncertainty = FeatureCovariance(), when their covariance was singular or NaN (no detection without a usable covariance is returned).\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> plan = AsiftPlan(64, 48; tilts = :untilted);\n\njulia> img = zeros(Float32, 64, 48);   # (W, H): x along the first axis\n\njulia> asift_detect!(plan, img)\nImasFeatureKA[]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.AsiftPlan"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._chunk_warps-Tuple{Any, Int64, Int64, Integer}","page":"Detection","text":"_chunk_warps(dev, nw, warp_bytes, chunk) -> Int\n\nWarps to keep resident per chunk. chunk > 0 is honoured verbatim (capped at nw); chunk <= 0 auto-sizes from device_free_bytes so the pyramids fit in _PYRAMID_BUDGET of what is free right now, less the room ONNX Runtime's CUDA sessions may take (_onnx_cuda_reserve).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._chunk_warps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detect!-Tuple{AsiftPlan, AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}","page":"Detection","text":"asift_detect!(plan, img_xy; nms=:none, window_half=3, cutoff=3.0,\n              polarity=:none, band_octaves=0.0, band_reach=1,\n              intensity_thr=0.04, edge_thr=10.0,\n              boundary_K=3.0, uncertainty=nothing) -> Vector{ImasFeatureKA}\n\nFull batched IMAS-25 detection in the plan's preallocated buffers. With nms = :mxm or :ellipse the cross-warp NMS runs on-device and only the survivors are returned; :none returns the full boundary-filtered pool. The pool is sorted into a canonical order first (position, scale, response, then every other field), so the features and their order do not depend on the threads or the device's scheduling. polarity (:dark → response > 0, :light → response < 0) gates the pool BEFORE the NMS splat, so a blob of the other sign can never suppress a kept one. band_octaves > 0 restricts suppression to features of comparable scale: a feature in scale band b = ⌊log2(√det S) / band_octaves⌋ can only be suppressed by features in bands b ± band_reach (see _band_pool!); 0 is the whole-pool rule. The seed blur σ₀ — the pyramid's, and the one the reported σ is scaled by — is the plan's (AsiftPlan(W, H; seed_sigma)).\n\nuncertainty = FeatureCovariance() also returns each feature's covariance: the result is then (; features, cov), cov[i] the FeatureCovariance covariance of features[i], a CovMat{3} over (x, y, log σ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detect!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.ImasFeatureKA","page":"Detection","text":"ImasFeatureKA\n\nOne raw feature of the ASIFT detector, as asift_detect! and asift_detect(img; raw = true) return it, before conversion to a Detection by asift_detections. All fields are in the input image's frame:\n\nx, y: the refined centre in 0-indexed OpenCV pixel coordinates (top-left pixel centre at (0, 0), 0.5 less than the COLMAP coordinates of a Detection);\nσ: the characteristic scale, in input pixels;\nintensity: the DoG response at the extremum, positive for a dark blob and negative for a light one;\ns_xx, s_xy, s_yy: the symmetric 2×2 shape factor of the elliptic frame, back-projected from the tilted warp it was found in;\nwarp_idx: the index of that warp in the plan's tilt schedule (1 is the identity).\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> f = ImasFeatureKA(10.0, 20.0, 2.0, -0.1, 2.0, 0.0, 2.0, 1);\n\njulia> d = only(asift_detections([f]));\n\njulia> d.center, is_light(d)\n([10.5, 20.5], true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.ImasFeatureKA"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.tilt_schedule-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Detection","text":"tilt_schedule(name::Symbol) -> Vector{Tuple{Float64, Float64}}\ntilt_schedule(tilts) -> Vector{Tuple{Float64, Float64}}\n\nThe simulated camera tilts ASIFT detects under, as (t, φ) pairs (tilt and longitude in radians), by name or as given (tilts, any collection of pairs):\n\n:imas25 — IMAS-25 (the default of asift_detect and AsiftPlan): the identity, 8 tilts of 2.62 and 16 of 5.18 at evenly spaced longitudes. Affine invariance for wide-baseline views.\n:untilted — the identity warp alone, [(1.0, 0.0)]: plain DoG-SIFT. Its keypoints localize best: on real panorama frames a tilted warp's keypoints land about 0.95 px rms from the untilted view's of the same structure, the untilted's about 0.3 px from debiased SIFT, so for views that differ by a rotation (a panorama) it is the better schedule.\n\nAny other name is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.tilt_schedule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.FeatureCovariance","page":"Detection","text":"FeatureCovariance()\n\nAsk asift_detect for each keypoint's 3×3 covariance over (x, y, log σ), by the implicit function theorem, for iid unit pixel noise.\n\nasift_detect(img; uncertainty = FeatureCovariance()) returns detections whose frame is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Uncertain(frame, Σ̃); the default uncertainty = nothing runs the detector unchanged. cov(d) is Σ̃, feature_frame(d) the frame, and d.center, d.σ and the metadata read as on any detection.\n\nModel. A refined DoG keypoint is a stationary point of the detector's local quadratic model of the DoG in (slice, x, y). With Ĥ its finite-difference Hessian and C the covariance, under unit iid pixel noise, of that model's gradient at the keypoint, Σ̃ = J·Ĥ⁻¹·C·Ĥ⁻¹·Jᵀ: C is tabulated exactly for the discrete pyramid (warp, upsample, blurs, octave downsamples) once per plan, and J maps to the reported coordinates, then through the ASIFT back-projection.\n\nParameterization. Σ̃ is a CovMat{3} over (x, y, log σ) in the detection's image frame (COLMAP pixel centres, as d.center), σ = d.σ (the frame's characteristic scale √det S), log natural. Position-only consumers take the top-left 2×2 block (the marginal). A tilted ASIFT warp's frame has the shape S = σ_w·M_w with M_w fixed by the warp (d.warp_idx into the tilt schedule), so (x, y, log σ) are its only free parameters and Σ̃ is full rank.\n\nUnits. Unit noise variance in the detector's intensity units: those of a float input image, and the [0, 1] scale for 8-bit input (UInt8, N0f8, …; σn = σgray / 255). The physical covariance is σ_n²·Σ̃.\n\nEvery detection is refined, and has a covariance: the detector searches the interior scale slices only and drops an extremum whose sub-pixel refinement fails, and with FeatureCovariance it drops, exactly so, a detection whose covariance is singular or NaN (the implicit-function system singular: not finite and positive definite), before the cross-warp NMS; plan.refine_dropped counts both. Every returned keypoint's Σ̃ is finite and positive definite.\n\nExtension point (not implemented): an intensity-dependent noise model, e.g. FeatureCovariance(noise = ShotNoise(a, b)), makes the middle term K·diag(σ²(I))·Kᵀ, gathered from the pyramid per keypoint instead of tabulated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.FeatureCovariance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gaussian_taps-Tuple{Real}","page":"Detection","text":"gaussian_taps(sep_sigma) -> Vector{Float32}\n\nOne-sided normalized Gaussian taps [k0, k1, …, kr] (center + r taps), with r = max(1, ceil(4·sep_sigma)). Normalized so k0 + 2·Σ kᵢ = 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gaussian_taps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.sift_nb_octaves-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Detection","text":"sift_nb_octaves(W, H; upsample=false) -> Int\n\nAuto octave count: floor(log2(min(W,H))) − 4 (+1 if upsampling).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.sift_nb_octaves"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.sift_sep_sigmas-Tuple{Real, Real, Integer}","page":"Detection","text":"sift_sep_sigmas(seed_sigma, input_blur, nb_scales) -> Vector{Float32}\n\nPer-level separable-blur σ for one octave (nbscales+3 levels). Level 0 lifts the assumed `inputblurup toseedsigma`; each subsequent level advances by a factor 2^(1/nbscales) in absolute scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.sift_sep_sigmas"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.imas_rotated_canvas-Tuple{Integer, Integer, Real}","page":"Detection","text":"Rotated-canvas geometry for a θ rotation of an nx×ny image (Float32, matches C).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.imas_rotated_canvas"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.cross_warp_nms-Tuple{AbstractVector{ImasFeatureKA}, Integer, Integer}","page":"Detection","text":"cross_warp_nms(feats, W, H; device, mode=:mxm, window_half=3, cutoff=3.0,\n               band_octaves=0.0, band_reach=1) -> Vector{ImasFeatureKA}\n\nCross-warp NMS over an aggregated feature pool (input-frame). mode is :mxm (centroid window window_half) or :ellipse (Mahalanobis footprint, cutoff cutoff). band_octaves > 0 restricts suppression to features of comparable scale — band ⌊log2(√det S) / band_octaves⌋ ± band_reach. It runs the kernels asift_detect! runs on its device pool, on the pool in the same canonical order (exact ties in the quantized response break toward the later feature in that order), so the survivors do not depend on the order of feats, and on the pool asift_detect(img; nms = :none, raw = true) returns it keeps exactly the features asift_detect(img; nms = mode, raw = true) keeps, given the same detection keywords. Returns the survivors in that canonical order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.cross_warp_nms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.spread_strongest-Tuple{Any, Any, Integer}","page":"Detection","text":"spread_strongest(detections, image_size, n; tolerance = 0.1, device = default_device()) -> detections\n\nAbout n of the strongest detections spread over the image: Suppression via Square Covering (SSC; Bailo et al., Pattern Recognition Letters 106, 2018, ported from the authors' code, https://github.com/BAILOOL/ANMS-Codes, MIT). In descending |response| (ties: the earlier detection first), a detection is kept unless a kept one lies in the square of side w about it (on a grid of cells of side w / 2); the side w is an integer number of pixels, found by the authors' binary search until the kept count is within round(n ∓ n·tolerance). The kept detections are returned in detector order.\n\nWhere the global top n by |response| would sit (on a frame with a bright window and a dim interior) nearly all in the window, this keeps the strongest of each neighbourhood, at whatever contrast the neighbourhood has.\n\nimage_size is (H, W) or a channelview's (C, H, W); the detections' centroids are in its COLMAP pixel frame ([0, W] × [0, H]). With n or fewer detections, detections itself is returned; n = 1 is the single strongest. Where no integer width lands in the band (the reference then returns its last covering, outside the band), the width is refined between the two bracketing integers by bisection, and if the count still jumps over the band, the covering above it loses its weakest detections down to the band's top; whenever the reference's search lands in the band, the result is the reference's. The count is below the band only when every covering is (fewer detections than the band can hold).\n\nOne KernelAbstractions implementation on the CPU or any GPU backend (device): the covering is computed in parallel (a per-cell maximum, then synchronous rounds of the greedy independent set of the cells in score order), and its result is the reference's sequential sweep's exactly, ties included; every backend returns the same set, run to run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.spread_strongest"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#The-Förstner-operator","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"detect_foerstner is the interest operator of Förstner and Gülch (Förstner and Gülch, 1987): it finds windows of a grey image whose gradients locate a point well, and estimates in each a junction point and a circular point by least squares, keeping the one a significance test prefers. It follows the MATLAB reference implementation ip_fop.m (Luxen, 2005) step by step, including the two extensions it takes from Köthe (Köthe, 2003): the image is processed at doubled resolution, and the :koethe window criterion ranks windows by the smaller eigenvalue of the structure tensor.\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\npoints  = detect_foerstner(img)                          # Float32, default_device()\ncorners = filter(d -> d.label === :corner, points)\nEllipse(first(corners); sigma = 1)                       # the 1σ error ellipse (ip_errell.m)","title":"The Förstner operator"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#What-it-returns","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"One row per window, a StructArray of Detection(Uncertain(PointFrame(x), Σ), (; label, window, F)). The frame is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's PointFrame, a position without a scale: neither point model estimates a size.\n\nlabel the row's point and covariance\n:corner the junction point (the reference's corner)\n:circular the reference's circular point (dot_point): the centre of a rotationally symmetric pattern, a dot or a ring. Förstner & Gülch's term; it carries no size estimate and is not a scale-space blob\n:unknown the window centre and a NaN covariance: the significance test labelled neither (the reference's noclass, which has no estimate)\n\nwindow is the window centre (the reference's win), and F the statistic Ω_corner / Ω_circular the label was decided on.","title":"What it returns"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#Coordinates","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"Positions are COLMAP pixel centres of the input image, (x, y) = (column, row), the top-left pixel's centre at (0.5, 0.5), as every detector of the package returns them. MATLAB indexes the pixel (r, c) at its centre, so a position (r, c) of the reference is (c − ½, r − ½) here.\n\nThe operator works on the doubled image. The package's one 2× upsampling (the ASIFT seed's, VLFeat's grid) makes the doubled pixel (r′, c′) (1-based) the bilinear sample at input position (r′/2, c′/2), 1-based, so a doubled-grid position is at COLMAP ((c′ − 1)/2, (r′ − 1)/2) in the input, and a covariance shrinks by 4. The conversion is made once, at the output.","title":"Coordinates"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#Devices-and-precision","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"The image-wide steps — the doubling, the gradient and its integration, the window measures, the regional maxima and the non-maximum suppression, and the two least-squares estimates per window — are KernelAbstractions kernels, the same code on KA.CPU() and on a GPU (device = CUDABackend()). The F-test and the result are assembled on the host. T sets the precision: Float32 by default, Float64 as the reference. On the reference's example image Quadrate.tif (500 × 500, 100 windows) a CUDA device and the CPU give the same labels and positions within 3.1 × 10⁻⁵ px in Float32 and 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁴ px in Float64; the test suite checks it whenever a CUDA device is present.","title":"Devices and precision"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#Accuracy","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"On area-sampled synthetic dots (radius 2.5 px, sub-pixel centres spread over the whole frame up to 8 px from every border) every circular point comes back within 0.0054 px of the truth. A junction is less exact: at an X-junction the window measure is flat around the vertex, so the window can land a few doubled pixels off it, and the point estimate in an off-centre window is biased by up to ≈ 0.1 px (within its reported covariance); this is the operator's, not a difference from the reference.\n\nOn Quadrate.tif with the defaults, both window criteria find 100 windows, all labelled :corner; with the reference example's parameters (noise_sigma = 1, integration = :gaussian, derivative_sigma = 0.7, integration_sigma = 2) also 100 corners, median position standard deviation 0.057 px.","title":"Accuracy"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#Differences-from-ip_fop.m","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"The implementation follows the reference line for line except in three places, each recorded here with its evidence.","title":"Differences from ip_fop.m"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#1.-Undoing-the-doubling:-fixed","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"The reference doubles the image with imresize(g, 2, 'bilinear') (line 172) and maps positions back with transf_H, transf_v (lines 340–350, applied at 436, 446 and 457): x = (1 − n)/(1 − 2n)·x′ + (1 − 2)n/(1 − 2n) per axis, n the image size. That map is the inverse of an align-corners grid, on which the doubled pixels 1 and 2n sit on the input pixels 1 and n. imresize's grid is not that: its doubled pixel j samples the input at j/2 + 1/4 (1-based), so the exact inverse is x = x′/2 + 1/4, and the reference's points are off by ((n + 1)/2 − x)/(2n − 1): zero at the image centre, up to 0.25 px toward the centre at the borders.\n\nWhat this rests on, since MATLAB cannot be run here: MATLAB's imresize documentation states that antialiasing applies only when shrinking, so a 2× bilinear enlargement is plain bilinear interpolation; and imresize.m maps output to input coordinates by u = x/scale + 0.5·(1 − 1/scale), i.e. u = x/2 + 1/4 for scale = 2, with the out-of-range neighbour of the first sample taken from the edge pixel (index mirroring, which at this offset replicates the border). The tests emulate that grid to measure the reference's map.\n\nMeasured on 30–42 area-sampled dots spread over the frame (sub-pixel centres, up to 8 px from every border), sampled on imresize's grid:\n\nback-map largest error\nthe reference's transf_H, transf_v 0.236 px (borders; ≈ 0 at the centre), matching the predicted ((n + 1)/2 − x)/(2n − 1) to 0.002 px at every dot\nimresize's exact inverse 0.0054 px\nthe package's upsampling grid, its exact inverse (what detect_foerstner does) 0.0054 px\n\nThe package's grid (input j/2) is a quarter input pixel from imresize's (input j/2 + 1/4); the doubled samples differ accordingly, and the measured effect on the positions is nil at this precision (0.0054 px either way).","title":"1. Undoing the doubling: fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#2.-The-circular-point's-residual-sum:-kept-as-the-reference-has-it","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"Line 423 computes the circular point's weighted residual sum as Σ γ_cc·Δr_dot² − 2γ_rc·Δr_corner·Δc_corner + γ_rr·Δc_corner²: two of its three terms use the junction model's residuals. Förstner and Gülch's circular model has, for each pixel i, the residual ∇gᵢ⊥ᵀ(x̂ − xᵢ) (the distance of the point from the line through the pixel along its gradient, weighted by the gradient), so its residual sum is Σ (xᵢ − x̂)ᵀ Γᵢ⊥ (xᵢ − x̂) with all three terms from the circular estimate. That sum enters the covariance Ω/redundancy · N⁻¹ (line 449) and the test statistic (line 427).\n\nA Monte Carlo test of the reported covariance does not support the derived form. On noisy synthetic dots (radius 2.5 px, random sub-pixel centres, 400 trials each; NEES eᵀΣ⁻¹e, nominal mean 2):\n\nnoise (of the [0, 1] range)  mean NEES 95 % coverage 99.9 % coverage\n0.01 line 423 (kept) 3.0 0.85 0.965\n0.01 derived residual sum 13.7 0.375 0.65\n0.03 line 423 (kept) 8.7 0.578 0.805\n0.03 derived residual sum 20.4 0.315 0.52\n\nNeither form is calibrated for circular points (both under-cover: the variance factor treats the gradients of a window as independent, which the smoothing makes them not), and the reference's form is the closer one. The labels were the same under both in all 1 600 trials (dots and X-junctions at both noise levels), the junction covariance does not involve it (NEES 0.91 and 1.72, 95 % coverage 0.998 and 0.96), and the reference's sum was never ≤ 0. So line 423 is kept as written; a test item reruns a 100-trial version of the comparison.","title":"2. The circular point's residual sum: kept as the reference has it"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#3.-Defaults:-the-code's","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"The reference's header documents SIGMA_N = 2.5 grey values and SIGMA_DIFF = 0.7 px (lines 37 and 50); its code runs with 2.0 and 1.0 (lines 521 and 524). The defaults here are the code's — what ip_fop.m actually ran. This is a conflict in the reference's documentation, not a correction.","title":"3. Defaults: the code's"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#Also-as-the-reference","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"noise_sigma is in grey levels of an 8-bit image, as the reference's for a uint8 image: the operator reads the image in [0, 1] and uses noise_sigma / 255. The reference stretches a floating-point image to its own range; this does not. With a Gaussian integration the estimation window still weighs its pixels equally, which the reference's header flags as an inconsistency.\n\nThe full docstring: detect_foerstner.","title":"Also as the reference"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/matching/#Matching","page":"Matching","text":"","title":"Matching"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/matching/#Nearest-neighbour-matching","page":"Matching","text":"nn_match and match compare two descriptor matrices, one descriptor per row, and return a Vector{NNMatch} sorted best first. Both run the same KernelAbstractions kernels: match on the CPU, nn_match on any device (device = CUDABackend(), for instance), and a GPU returns what the CPU returns. match is public but not exported, because it would shadow Base.match; call it as VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match.\n\nThe strategy is a positional argument:\n\nMutualNN (the default) keeps pairs that are each other's nearest neighbour;\nNN keeps every row's nearest neighbour;\nKnnMatch(k) keeps every row's k nearest neighbours, for a downstream geometric check to disambiguate.\n\nThe metric is a keyword: CosineSimilarity (the default, for unit descriptors) or L2. ratio_threshold applies Lowe's ratio test, and min_similarity or max_distance bound the score.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> D1 = Float32[1 0; 0 1; 0.6 0.8];\n\njulia> D2 = Float32[0 1; 1 0];\n\njulia> [(m.first, m.second) for m in nn_match(D1, D2)]\n2-element Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}:\n (1, 2)\n (2, 1)\n\njulia> length(nn_match(D1, D2, NN()))    # row 3 matches too, one way\n3","title":"Nearest-neighbour matching"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/matching/#Matches-as-correspondences","page":"Matching","text":"An NNMatch holds indices. csponds(features1, features2, matches) turns them into VisualGeometryToolkitCore correspondences between the matched features, with each match's score and ratio as attribute columns, ready for a robust estimator.\n\nDescribedFeature pairs a detection with its descriptor. described_features builds a StructArray of them from detections and a descriptor matrix, and descriptor_matrix returns the descriptors as a matrix without copying; match and csponds accept the StructArray directly.","title":"Matches as correspondences"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/matching/#Many-galleries-at-once","page":"Matching","text":"nn_match_stacked matches one set of features against many galleries (a dictionary of descriptor matrices) in one matrix product, with the same result per gallery as matching that gallery alone. A StackedDescriptorIndex keeps the stacked galleries on the device between calls.","title":"Many galleries at once"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/matching/#LoMa","page":"Matching","text":"LoMaDescriptor and LoMaMatcher run the learned DeDoDe descriptors and LoMa matchers of Nordström et al. (ECCV 2026) from the authors' ONNX exports, through ONNX Runtime on the CPU or CUDA. The models are downloaded on first use and cached in the package's scratch space. A descriptor describes about max_detections of the strongest detections, spread over the image (spread_strongest), of a 3 × H × W RGB channelview of any size (it resamples the image to the network's LOMA_IMAGE_SIZE² input itself, and the detections stay in the image's pixels); a matcher matches two described sets and returns Vector{NNMatch}, like match:\n\ndescribe = LoMaDescriptor(:b)\nmatcher = LoMaMatcher(:b128)\nfa = describe(img_a, asift_detect(img_a))\nfb = describe(img_b, asift_detect(img_b))\nmatches = matcher(fa, fb, size(img_a), size(img_b))\n\nBoth run on ONNX Runtime's CUDA provider when it is available and on the CPU otherwise (execution_provider = :auto, the default; the fallback warns and names why; :cpu and :cuda are explicit, and an explicit :cuda that cannot run is an error naming why). The CUDA provider needs a CUDA device and an environment that holds CUDA_Runtime_jll and CUDNN_jll: their CUDA 12 builds are downloaded on first use and loaded for ONNX Runtime alone, whatever CUDA runtime CUDA.jl uses (onnx_cuda_status says what was found). ONNX Runtime itself is, on the CUDA path, conda-forge's build of release 1.31.1 (Microsoft's GPU builds carry no code for sm100, the B200), with device code for sm70 to sm120; the package installs it on the first CUDA probe from four conda-forge files pinned by sha256 (0.34 GB, once per depot). ONNX Runtime's thread pool is threads = Threads.nthreads(), the process's allotment (in a Slurm job, start Julia with --threads set to the job's `SLURMCPUSPERTASK), never the machine's cores. [loma_provenance`](@ref) records the provider used, the threads and the library versions.\n\nAnother package's ONNX network runs on the same runtime through onnx_session(path; execution_provider, threads, gpu_mem_limit): the provider resolved as above, the thread pool the caller's, and on CUDA the session's device arena capped at gpu_mem_limit bytes (8 GiB by default), which the ASIFT detector's device plans leave free.\n\nThe package's examples/loma.jl runs this on a pair of downloaded images and draws the matches. Cite the LoMa and DeDoDe papers when you use these networks (their entries are on the home page).","title":"LoMa"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Developer-Notes","page":"Developer Notes","text":"This page is for people who work on the package itself. Users need none of it.","title":"Developer Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#The-repository","page":"Developer Notes","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures lives in the VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures/ directory of the VisualGeometryToolkit.jl repository, a Julia workspace of several packages with one Manifest at its root. Instantiate it once per clone, from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nThe repository ships git hooks in .githooks/ that re-resolve the root Manifest after a pull, checkout or rebase that changes a Project.toml. They are opt-in, once per clone:\n\ngit config core.hooksPath .githooks","title":"The repository"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Tests","page":"Developer Notes","text":"The suite uses ReTestItems; the items live in test/*_tests.jl. From the repository root:\n\njulia --project=VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\njulia --project=VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test(; test_args = [\"polarity\"])'\n\nThe second form runs the items whose names match the pattern. Check the total it reports: a pattern that matches nothing runs nothing.\n\ntest/readme_tests.jl checks that the README's install line names exactly the packages its example loads, that the landing page (docs/src/index.md) shows the same install line and the same example, and runs the example in a fresh environment built from the install line. Keep the example self-contained: no files, no network.","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Documentation","page":"Developer Notes","text":"Build from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures/docs --startup-file=no VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures/docs/make.jl\n\nThe build runs the doctests and checks the links, fails on any warning, and fails on a public docstring missing from the manual. test/docs_tests.jl fails on a public name without a docstring, a docstring detached from its definition, and a signature line no method accepts.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Benchmarks-and-examples","page":"Developer Notes","text":"benchmark/mc holds the Monte Carlo studies of the detector, which are not part of the test suite: keypoint_uncertainty_mc.jl calibrates the keypoint covariance, and zeisl_comparison.jl compares it with Zeisl et al. (BMVC 2009). Both write their results to benchmark/mc/results/, which is not tracked.\n\nexamples/ has its own environment, outside the workspace, so its plotting and image-loading dependencies stay out of the one workspace Manifest. It holds CUDA_Runtime_jll and CUDNN_jll (as the test environment does), whose CUDA 12 builds ONNX Runtime's CUDA provider takes (src/onnx_runtime.jl). The CUDA build of ONNX Runtime is conda-forge's, the artifact onnxruntime_cuda of Artifacts.toml (a tree hash only: the package installs it from the conda-forge files _ORT_CUDA_FILES pins by sha256, since Pkg cannot unpack a .conda). To move it to a new release, take the onnxruntime-cpp cuda12x build and the libprotobuf, libabseil and libre2-11 builds its metadata requires, update their names and sha256s, and pin the tree hash the merged install gives. Instantiate it with julia --project=VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures/examples -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'.","title":"Benchmarks and examples"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Accuracy-bounds-that-other-code-relies-on","page":"Developer Notes","text":"The refinement accuracy pinned by the test suite (two detections of the same small, isolated blob differ by at most 0.06 px) is what VisualGeometryToolkitCore's coincidence gate is built around. That gate, COINCIDENT_PX = 0.1 px, supplied as tol_px in Core's src/geometry/model_validity.jl, rejects minimal samples with coincident points, and it is set to clear the 0.06 px bound with margin. A change to the refinement that loosens the bound therefore changes which samples Core's estimators reject. Coordinate it with Core instead of retuning the test here.","title":"Accuracy bounds that other code relies on"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Serialization","page":"Developer Notes","text":"The package writes no file format. Detections, feature frames, Size2 and quantities are serialized by VisualGeometryToolkitIO (json_value, from_json), which owns the stack's quantity JSON format: numbers in the record's declared units. The {\"value\", \"unit\"} objects this package's JSON.jl hooks once wrote are not read; a file holding them is migrated once (vgio_migrate).","title":"Serialization"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"This page installs the package and walks through the README's example one step at a time. Every Julia block on this page is a doctest, run by the docs build.","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"Install VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures from the General registry (Julia 1.12 or later):\n\npkg> add VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\nThe geometric types come from VisualGeometryToolkitCore, which is installed as a dependency. Add it to your environment as well (pkg> add VisualGeometryToolkitCore) when you construct frames or points yourself.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#A-synthetic-image","page":"Getting Started","text":"The detector takes an H×W matrix: Gray pixels, UInt8, or real values in [0, 1]. The image below is a gray field with three Gaussian blobs, each given as (row, column, σ, amplitude). A positive amplitude makes a light blob and a negative one a dark blob.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> blobs = [(40, 50, 4.0, 0.35), (70, 100, 3.0, -0.3), (30, 130, 6.0, 0.3)];\n\njulia> img = [0.5 + sum(a * exp(-((r - y)^2 + (c - x)^2) / (2s^2)) for (y, x, s, a) in blobs)\n              for r in 1:120, c in 1:160];\n\njulia> size(img)\n(120, 160)","title":"A synthetic image"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Detecting-features","page":"Getting Started","text":"asift_detect runs the IMAS-25 ASIFT detector: the image is warped by 25 simulated camera tilts, DoG extrema are found in each warp, and the detections are mapped back to the input image and merged by a cross-warp non-maximum suppression. Each result is a Detection: an elliptic feature frame with the detector's metadata.\n\njulia> dets = asift_detect(img);\n\njulia> length(dets)\n3\n\njulia> dark = only(filter(is_dark, dets));\n\njulia> round.(dark.center; digits = 1)    # (x, y): column, then row\n2-element GeometryBasics.Point{2, Float64} with indices SOneTo(2):\n 99.5\n 69.5\n\njulia> round(dark.σ; digits = 1)\n3.0\n\njulia> count(is_light, dets)\n2\n\nThe centre is in COLMAP pixel coordinates, where the top-left pixel's centre is (0.5, 0.5). The blob placed at row 70 and column 100 is therefore found at (99.5, 69.5). Detection explains the coordinates, the accuracy of the sub-pixel refinement and the other options of the detector.\n\nThe metadata reads as properties of the detection:\n\njulia> propertynames(dark.metadata)\n(:response, :edge_score, :laplacian_scale_score, :warp_idx)\n\njulia> dark.response > 0    # a dark blob has a positive DoG response\ntrue","title":"Detecting features"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Matching-descriptors","page":"Getting Started","text":"nn_match matches two descriptor matrices, one descriptor per row. By default it keeps mutual nearest neighbours under the cosine similarity, so the descriptors should have unit length. Here the second set is the first, shuffled and perturbed:\n\njulia> using Random\n\njulia> rng = Xoshiro(1);\n\njulia> unit_rows(D) = D ./ sqrt.(sum(abs2, D; dims = 2));\n\njulia> D1 = unit_rows(randn(rng, Float32, 100, 32));\n\njulia> perm = randperm(rng, 100);\n\njulia> D2 = unit_rows(D1[perm, :] .+ 0.05f0 .* randn(rng, Float32, 100, 32));\n\njulia> matches = nn_match(D1, D2);\n\njulia> length(matches)\n100\n\njulia> all(perm[m.second] == m.first for m in matches)\ntrue\n\nEach match is an NNMatch: m.first indexes the rows of D1, m.second those of D2, and m.score and m.ratio are the similarity and Lowe's ratio. Matching covers the other strategies and metrics, the GPU and the learned LoMa matchers.","title":"Matching descriptors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Saving-detections","page":"Getting Started","text":"Detections are written to and read from JSON by VisualGeometryToolkitIO (json_value(d, contract; kind) and from_json(Detection, obj, contract; kind)), which owns the stack's quantity JSON format; its Serialization guide describes the format.","title":"Saving detections"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#Raw-images","page":"Raw images","text":"Julia has no convention for raw (Bayer) images: JuliaImages reads none, and BayeredImages.jl (unregistered) wraps a plain matrix as a CFAImage <: AbstractMatrix with a periodic colour filter array. The stack keeps its own types, in this package, as its one raw representation, and takes one idea from BayeredImages: the raw wrapper IS an AbstractMatrix, so an ordinary array or image function works on it unchanged. There is no dependency on BayeredImages.","title":"Raw images"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#The-representation","page":"Raw images","text":"A decoded file is a RawMosaic{A} <: AbstractMatrix{UInt16}: the photosite matrix A (an Array, a CuArray, or a view of either), its colour pattern anchored at the full raw frame's origin, the visible area's origin in that frame, the black and white levels and the EXIF metadata.\n\nsize, getindex, IndexStyle and parent are the held array's.\nWhat an array function makes from it is a plain array of the held array's kind: similar, copy, a broadcast (m .- black), a reduction (sum, extrema). The colour pattern and the metadata stay with the mosaic, and nothing pretends a derived array still has them.\nA unit-range index m[rows, columns] (a copy) or view(m, rows, columns) (no copy) is a window: a RawMosaic of the same sensor whose window_origin is its offset into the visible area. Its colours are the parent's at those photosites, whatever the parity of its start (the phase shift BayeredImages makes with a circshift of the pattern is the offset here, added where the pattern is tiled). Any other index is a plain array.\nOn a device: adapt(CuArray, m) or adapt(backend, m) (a KernelAbstractions backend) moves the photosites and keeps the rest. A kernel receives parent(m), never the wrapper (its metadata is not a device value): the cell kernel of LinearRaw runs on the photosites' device and leaves its planes and saturation flags there.\n\nA LinearRaw{Mode} (:green_cells, :bin2, :demosaic) stays a container of plain planes, L.image[row, column, plane], beside its saturation flags, geometry, gain and metadata. Its planes are not wrappers that each know their BayerCellFrame{C}, for three reasons:\n\nEvery caller reads the planes as one array: a warp reads all of them in one call (image_warp!(dst, warps, L.image), a tuple of one warp per plane), the decode stage writes L.image as one PNG, variance_stabilize takes a view of one plane, and a quantile reads vec(L.image). A tuple of plane wrappers would split that array or make each caller reassemble it.\nA plane's frame is already one call away and needs nothing new: bayer_plane_map(L, c) and raw_frame_map(L) (the green plane, the cell grid).\nThe saturation flags, the gain and the green code spans belong to the cell, not to one plane; per-plane wrappers would hold copies of them or point back at the container.\n\nThe mosaic, by contrast, is one matrix, and the wrapper costs its callers nothing.","title":"The representation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#Where-an-image's-sensor-pixel-coordinates-come-from","page":"Raw images","text":"ONE accessor: raw_coordinates(img, x), the point x of the image's pixel frame (COLMAP: pixel (row, column) centred at (column − ½, row − ½)) in the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}, the frame every calibration of the raw is in. It is raw_frame_map(img)(x):\n\nimage raw_frame_map(img) raw_coordinates(img, x)\na RawMosaic as decoded the identity x\na window of a mosaic its offset x + (columns, rows) of window_origin\na LinearRaw cell image (:green_cells, :bin2) the cell grid's map 2x (then a window's offset)\na :demosaic image refused (ArgumentError) refused\na plain matrix no method no method: it states no frame\n\nA position on another plane of a :bin2 image is bayer_plane_map(L, c)(x): the planes are sampled at different points of the cell, so each has its own frame, and a composition that joins two planes' frames is refused.","title":"Where an image's sensor-pixel coordinates come from"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#How-detectors-report-positions","page":"Raw images","text":"A detector works in the pixels of the image it is given and returns COLMAP pixel centres of that image. A caller that runs it on a raw image and writes positions as raw pixels maps them through the image first:\n\nL = load_raw(path; mode = :green_cells)\nxs = detect(L.image[:, :, 1])                  # any detector: COLMAP centres of the cell grid\nraw_xs = raw_coordinates.(Ref(L), xs)          # the sensor's pixels\n\nWriting the cell-grid positions as raw pixels unmapped puts them at half the scale; RadiometricCamera's chrome detection did exactly that with green-cell coordinates. A calibration matrix moves the same way, through Core: reframed_calibration(raw_frame_map(L), K_cells).","title":"How detectors report positions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/raw-images/#How-the-frame-survives-a-file-round-trip","page":"Raw images","text":"A file of a decoded image (a PNG of the cells) holds pixels and no frame, and a plain matrix read back from it has no raw_coordinates. The stage that writes it records the frame with it: raw_provenance(L) gives the decoder, the mode, the gain and the frame map as plain data (from, to, scale, translation), for the stage's record (RadiometricCamera's conversion.json). The stage that reads the file rebuilds the map from that record and maps every position it writes through it.\n\nNext step: RadiometricCamera's decode stage records raw_provenance(L) per photograph (today its record holds the size and gain, not the map), and a reader of a provenance record returns the SimilarityMap it states, so a stage reading the PNG asks raw_coordinates of the decoded record rather than assuming a scale.","title":"How the frame survives a file round trip"},{"category":"section","location":"api/image/#Image-Input","page":"Image Input","text":"The image-input helpers, the linear-radiance PNG reader (load_linear_image) and the camera RAW reader (load_raw).","title":"Image Input"},{"category":"section","location":"api/image/#Camera-RAW","page":"Image Input","text":"The stack's one RAW path: every consumer reads RAW files through this module, and VisualGeometryToolkitCore's check_raw_decoding flags any other decoding path. load_raw(path; mode, gain) reads a Bayer RAW file in the sensor's orientation (the EXIF flip is never applied). The decoder is LibRawDecoder (using LibRaw_jll: LibRaw 0.22 through ccall, no Python). Everything after the decode is Julia code here. A decoded RawMosaic is an AbstractMatrix of its photosites, and raw_coordinates is every image's one way to its sensor pixels: the guide's Raw images page is the design.\n\nmode image use\n:green_cells each 2×2 Bayer cell's two greens, averaged; half resolution, one plane geometry and radiometry\n:bin2 the same cells with their red and blue (R, the green-cell plane, B) colour radiometry\n:demosaic LibRaw's AHD demosaic, full resolution, linear display figures only\n\nThe cell modes are exact: (raw − black[c]) / (white − black[c]) × gain, with saturation flags and each plane's true sample position. Their pixel frame, the cell grid BayerCellFrame{:G}, maps onto the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap} by raw_frame_map, x ↦ 2x exactly, for every Bayer phase and either parity of the visible origin. Its inverse is inv, and a calibration matrix moves with it through Core's reframed_calibration(raw_frame_map(L), K). Each plane is a frame of its own, BayerCellFrame{C}, and bayer_plane_map(L, c) is its map onto the raw: the plane's translation onto the cell grid ((±¼, ±¼) cells for R and B, none for G) composed with raw_frame_map(L). A warp reads plane c through inv(bayer_plane_map(L, c)) ∘ math like any other map, all planes in one call (image_warp!(dst, warps, L.image), a tuple of one warp per plane); a composition that joins two planes' frames is refused. A green-cell value is the mean of two diagonal photosites, at (±½, ∓½) raw pixels from the cell centre for an RGGB or BGGR cell and (±½, ±½) for GRBG or GBRG; green_cell_sites gives them, so a renderer can model the footprint exactly. The demosaic interpolates. It has no frame map, so the geometric accessors refuse it. raw_provenance records the decoder, LibRaw's version, the mode and the frame map.\n\nThe EXIF fields become VisualGeometryToolkitCore's typed values through exposure_settings, lens_settings, body_identity, lens_identity and capture_time, from a mosaic or from load_raw_metadata (the headers only); extract_capture_settings gathers a capture's frames into a CaptureSettings. A lens's EXIF name is a claim: on an adapter (the FX3 with Sigma EF lenses) it names another lens, so a pipeline that knows its lens states it.","title":"Camera RAW"},{"category":"section","location":"api/image/#The-detection-image-of-a-RAW","page":"Image Input","text":"variance_stabilize maps the green cells to the generalized Anscombe transform for Poisson–Gaussian noise: the noise has σ ≈ 1 at every level, so ASIFT on it (asift_detect of a StabilizedImage) thresholds its DoG at noise_sigmas noise σ, in a dim interior as in a bright window, and masks the saturated pixels. A descriptor still reads a display rendering of the same cells: both are pixel-wise, so the positions coincide.","title":"The detection image of a RAW"},{"category":"section","location":"api/image/#Image-input,-the-linear-PNG-reader-and-the-display-encoding","page":"Image Input","text":"A linear image is shown through one encoding, srgb_image: exposure, a white balance per plane, an optional tone curve, clamp, the sRGB transfer curve (linear_to_srgb, the inverse of srgb_to_linear) and 8 or 16 bits, a NaN pixel the background.","title":"Image input, the linear PNG reader and the display encoding"},{"category":"type","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.BayerCellFrame","page":"Image Input","text":"BayerCellFrame{C} <: Frame{2}\n\nThe COLMAP pixel frame of one colour plane C (:R, :G or :B) of the half-resolution grid of 2×2 Bayer cells — the planes of the :green_cells and :bin2 images of LinearRaw: pixel (row, column) of the plane is centred at (column − ½, row − ½) of its frame. The planes are indexed alike but sampled at different points of the cell, so each is a frame of its own: bayer_plane_map maps plane C's frame onto the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}, and a composition that joins one plane's frame to another's is refused (MethodError), as at every frame junction of Core. BayerCellFrame{:G} is the cell grid itself (the greens' mean sits at the cell centre in every phase): raw_frame_map is its map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.BayerCellFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LibRawDecoder","page":"Image Input","text":"LibRawDecoder()\n\nDecode with LibRaw through LibRawjll (`using LibRawjll): accalllayer over LibRaw's C API, no Python. The extension checks LibRaw's version at load and the layout of every structure it reads against LibRaw's own accessors, and refuses (ErrorException`) a library it was not written for. The stack's one decoder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LibRawDecoder"},{"category":"type","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LinearRaw","page":"Image Input","text":"LinearRaw\n\nA RAW image as linear values: (raw − black[c]) / (white − black[c]) × gain, camera raw colour, white balance 1, 1.0 = the white level (before the gain). Not clipped: a value above 1 (a gain above 1) or below 0 (noise under the black level) is kept.\n\nimage::AbstractArray{Float64, 3} — image[row, column, plane], the planes plain arrays on the mosaic's device (an Array on the host); a plane's frame is asked of the image with its index (bayer_plane_map(L, c)).\nsaturated::AbstractMatrix{Bool} (a BitMatrix on the host) — the pixels whose value includes a photosite at the white level (raw ≥ white): in :bin2 a cell is saturated when ANY of its four photosites is, so a cell whose two greens straddle the white level is flagged rather than averaged into a plausible value; in :green_cells when either green is.\ngeometry — where the samples are, (; plane_maps, green_offsets, green_dn):\nplane_maps, per plane, its map onto the visible raw (bayer_plane_map, which reads it);\ngreen_offsets, the cell's two green photosites, (Δx, Δy) from the cell centre in RAW pixels (green_cell_sites);\ngreen_dn, the two green photosites' white − black[c], the codes that span 0 … 1 (before the gain), in the order of green_offsets: what a value's photon and read noise scale by (variance_stabilize).\nnothing for :demosaic, whose pixels are interpolated, not samples.\ngain and the mosaic's metadata (the decoder and LibRaw's version among it).\n\nThe mode is the type's first parameter, LinearRaw{:green_cells}, LinearRaw{:bin2} or LinearRaw{:demosaic}: the geometric accessors are methods of the cell images, and the display image's are its refusals. raw_provenance gives the mode, the gain, the metadata and the frame map as plain data for a result to record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LinearRaw"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LinearRaw-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Image Input","text":"LinearRaw(mosaic::RawMosaic; mode::Symbol, gain::Real = 1) -> LinearRaw\n\nThe mosaic's linear values, (raw − black[c]) / (white − black[c]) × gain, on the half-resolution grid of 2×2 Bayer cells (H ÷ 2 × W ÷ 2; an odd last row or column is dropped), arranged by mode (required: there is no neutral choice between them):\n\n:green_cells — THE image for geometry and radiometry, one plane: each cell's two green photosites' mean. The greens of a cell are always diagonal, so in every Bayer phase their mean sits at the cell centre (offset (0, 0)), and the cell grid maps onto the raw exactly by raw_frame_map (x ↦ 2x in COLMAP coordinates). The footprint of a value is its two photosites, at (±½, ∓½) raw pixels from the cell centre for an RGGB or BGGR cell, (±½, ±½) for GRBG or GBRG (green_cell_sites).\n:bin2 — the same cells with their red and blue, × 3 planes: R, the mean of the two greens (the :green_cells image, bit for bit), B. R and B sit at their photosites, (±¼, ±¼) cell pixels from the centre: each plane's map onto the raw is bayer_plane_map.\n\n:demosaic (LibRaw's interpolation, for display figures) needs the file: load_raw(path; mode = :demosaic).\n\nThe values are those convert_raw.py writes (--mode bin2), in the same Float64 arithmetic and order, before its clip to [0, 1] and 16-bit rounding.\n\nSaturation is raw ≥ white with no margin: on the Sony ARW sets this was written for, codes pile up exactly at the white level (16383) while the histogram runs smoothly through the camera's declared linear limit (15360, metadata.linear_max), so any margin would be a tuned constant. A caller who wants that stricter limit thresholds mosaic.raw itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LinearRaw"},{"category":"type","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.RawDecoder","page":"Image Input","text":"RawDecoder\n\nA RAW decoder, the token load_raw and load_raw_mosaic dispatch on: LibRawDecoder, implemented by a package extension that needs its trigger package loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.RawDecoder"},{"category":"type","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.RawMosaic","page":"Image Input","text":"RawMosaic{A <: AbstractMatrix{UInt16}, M <: NamedTuple} <: AbstractMatrix{UInt16}\n\nA RAW file decoded and nothing more: the visible area of the sensor, as LibRaw reports it. It IS its photosite matrix: size, indexing and parent are the held array's, so an ordinary array or image function reads it unchanged, and what such a function makes (similar, copy, a broadcast, a reduction) is a plain array of the held array's kind — the colour pattern and the metadata stay with the mosaic. copy, ==, isequal and isapprox are the held array's own (no element read through the wrapper, so a device mosaic compares on the device), and equality is the photosite matrix's: two mosaics with the same photosites are == whatever their colour pattern, window origin or metadata (compare photosite_colors, window_origin and metadata for those), and a mosaic is == to its held array. It is read-only (no setindex!): write to parent(m). The held array may be a device array (adapt(CuArray, m), or adapt(backend, m) with a KernelAbstractions backend, moves the photosites and keeps the rest); a kernel receives parent(m), never the wrapper.\n\nA unit-range index m[rows, columns] (a copy) or view(m, rows, columns) (no copy) is a window: a mosaic of its own whose colours are the parent's at those photosites (the colour phase shifts with an odd start, as a circshift of the pattern would) and whose raw_frame_map is its offset into the visible raw. Any other index is a plain array.\n\nraw::A — the photosites, raw[row, column], in the sensor's orientation (no EXIF flip): pixel (row, column) is the raw's COLMAP pixel centred at (column − ½, row − ½) — the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}, the frame every calibration of the raw is in (a window's pixels are offset by window_origin).\npattern::Matrix{UInt8} — the smallest repeating colour pattern, square, at most 4 × 4, from the full raw frame's origin (LibRaw's COLOR read from there), in LibRaw's colour indices: 0 R, 1 G, 2 B, 3 the second G of an RGBG pattern. Each photosite's index is photosite_colors(mosaic), the pattern tiled from that origin.\nblack::NTuple{4, Float64} — the black level per colour index (the common and per-channel parts summed, LibRaw's adjust_bl).\nwhite::Float64 — the white level (LibRaw's maximum): the code at which a photosite is saturated.\nvisible_origin::NTuple{2, Int} — (top_margin, left_margin), the visible area's offset into the full raw frame, which is raw_size ((height, width)).\nmetadata::NamedTuple — the EXIF fields (datetime_original, subsec, exposure_s, fnumber, focal_mm, iso, model, make, body_serial, lens_make, lens_model, lens_serial, focus_distance_m; nothing where absent, a non-finite or non-positive focus distance too; typed by exposure_settings, lens_settings, body_identity, lens_identity and capture_time), daylight_wb (LibRaw's daylight white balance, R G B), linear_max (the camera's declared linear-response limit per channel, nothing when the file has none), decoder and libraw_version.\nwindow_origin::NTuple{2, Int} — (rows, columns), the offset of raw[1, 1] from the visible area's first photosite: (0, 0) as decoded (the keyword of the constructor), a window's start less one otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.RawMosaic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.bayer_plane_map-Tuple{LinearRaw, Integer}","page":"Image Input","text":"bayer_plane_map(L::LinearRaw, c::Integer) -> SimilarityMap{2, …, BayerCellFrame{C}, PixelFrame{:colmap}}\n\nThe Bayer transform of plane c of the image: the map of the plane's own frame (BayerCellFrame{C}, C its colour — :R, :G, :B for :bin2, :G for :green_cells) onto the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}. It is the plane's translation onto the cell grid (where its samples sit from the cell centre: (±¼, ±¼) cell pixels for R and B, none for G) composed with raw_frame_map(L), so pixel (row, column) of the plane, at (column − ½, row − ½) of its frame, goes onto the centre of the photosites its value is the mean of — R's or B's one, the two greens' midpoint — exactly, for every Bayer phase and either parity of the visible origin. The green plane's map IS raw_frame_map(L).\n\nA warp reads plane c of an image through inv(bayer_plane_map(L, c)) ∘ math, math landing in the raw's pixels, like any other map (VisualGeometryToolkitCore.image_warp; one warp per plane: image_warp!(dst, warps, L.image) with a tuple of them). A composition that joins two planes' frames is refused (MethodError). A plane past the image's, or a :demosaic image, is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.bayer_plane_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.body_identity-Tuple{Union{NamedTuple, RawMosaic}}","page":"Image Input","text":"body_identity(x) -> DeviceIdentity\n\nThe camera body a RAW file says took it (make, model, body_serial), as Core's DeviceIdentity; a field the file does not state is nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.body_identity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.capture_time-Tuple{Union{NamedTuple, RawMosaic}}","page":"Image Input","text":"capture_time(x) -> Union{String, Nothing}\n\nWhen a RAW frame was taken, as the file states it: EXIF's DateTimeOriginal (local time, no zone) with its SubSecTimeOriginal, written ISO 8601 (\"2026-10-29T14:03:12.34\"); nothing when the file states none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.capture_time"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.cfa_string-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Image Input","text":"cfa_string(mosaic::RawMosaic) -> String\n\nThe colour pattern as letters, row by row (\"RGGB\" for [0 1; 3 2]): LibRaw's indices read through \"RGBG\", as convert_raw.py records it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.cfa_string"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.exposure_settings-Tuple{Union{NamedTuple, RawMosaic}}","page":"Image Input","text":"exposure_settings(x) -> ExposureSettings\n\nThe exposure a RAW frame was taken with — x a RawMosaic or its metadata (load_raw_metadata) — as Core's ExposureSettings: exposure_s seconds, fnumber, iso. A field the file does not state is an ArgumentError naming it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.exposure_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.extract_capture_settings-Tuple{AbstractDict{<:Integer}}","page":"Image Input","text":"extract_capture_settings(frames; lens = nothing) -> CaptureSettings\n\nThe camera settings of a capture from its RAW files' EXIF: frames maps a frame index to a RawMosaic or its metadata (load_raw_metadata); each frame's settings are exposure_settings and lens_settings. The body is the frames' body_identity, which must be one (identities agree when no field both state differs, case aside; ArgumentError otherwise). The lens is lens where the caller states it — a config knows an adapted lens EXIF misnames — and the frames' one EXIF claim otherwise (lens_identity, which must then agree across the frames); a stated lens that disagrees with EXIF is used, with a warning naming both. EXIF's claim stays readable from the metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.extract_capture_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.green_cell_sites-Tuple{LinearRaw, Integer, Integer}","page":"Image Input","text":"green_cell_sites(L::LinearRaw, row::Integer, column::Integer) -> NTuple{2, SVector{2, Float64}}\n\nThe footprint of the green-cell value of pixel (row, column): the centres of its two green photosites in the visible raw's COLMAP frame. They are diagonal, at (±½, ∓½) raw pixels from the cell centre raw_frame_map(L)((column − ½, row − ½)) in an RGGB or BGGR cell and (±½, ±½) in a GRBG or GBRG one, and the value is the mean of the two — what a renderer models to predict it exactly. A :demosaic image is refused (ArgumentError).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.green_cell_sites"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.lens_identity-Tuple{Union{NamedTuple, RawMosaic}}","page":"Image Input","text":"lens_identity(x) -> DeviceIdentity\n\nThe lens a RAW file CLAIMS took it (lens_make, lens_model, lens_serial). Kept, not trusted: a lens on an adapter is named by what the adapter reports (the FX3 with Sigma EF lenses claims \"DT 8mm F4 SAM\"), so a pipeline that knows its lens states it (extract_capture_settings's lens).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.lens_identity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.lens_settings-Tuple{Union{NamedTuple, RawMosaic}}","page":"Image Input","text":"lens_settings(x) -> LensSettings\n\nThe lens settings a RAW frame was taken with, as Core's LensSettings: the focal length the lens reports (focal_mm, the NOMINAL value, not a calibration) and the focus distance where the file states a finite, positive one (nothing otherwise — the FX3's adapted lenses report infinity). A file without a focal length is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.lens_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.load_raw-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Image Input","text":"load_raw(path; mode::Symbol, gain::Real = 1, white_balance = nothing, decoder::RawDecoder = LibRawDecoder(),\n         cache = nothing) -> LinearRaw\n\nA camera RAW file as linear values, in the sensor's orientation (the EXIF flip is never applied, in any mode). mode:\n\n:green_cells or :bin2 — exact: load_raw_mosaic, then LinearRaw(mosaic; mode, gain), on the half-resolution cell grid that raw_frame_map maps onto the raw. :green_cells is THE image for geometry and radiometry. Rounded to 16 bits (round(UInt16, clamp(x, 0, 1) * 65535)), :bin2 equals the linear PNG of RadiometricCamera's convert_raw.py (--mode bin2), value for value; unlike that PNG, saturated flags the cells that contain a photosite at the white level.\n:demosaic — FOR DISPLAY FIGURES ONLY. LibRaw's demosaic (AHD) of the visible area at full resolution: it INTERPOLATES the two colours each photosite did not record, so its values are not measurements and its pixels carry no exact geometry. Evaluation code must not read it; it has no frame map (raw_frame_map and green_cell_sites refuse it). LibRaw's output is linear (gamma 1, no auto-brightening, no maximum adjustment, 16 bits, the camera's raw colour, white balance 1); the white_balance (an R G B multiplier triple, e.g. from metadata.daylight_wb) and then the gain are applied here, in Julia. saturated flags pixels LibRaw clipped.\n\nwhite_balance is accepted with :demosaic only: the exact modes are camera raw colour, white balance 1.\n\ncache (a FileCache, using JLD2 provides it): each RAW is decoded once per content and settings — the entry is keyed by the file's SHA-256, mode, gain, white_balance, the decoder's type and its library's version (LibRaw's, libraw_version) (cache_key), so an edited file, another setting or an upgraded LibRaw decodes again and a hit returns the LinearRaw as decoded, value for value. nothing (the default) decodes every call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.load_raw"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.load_raw_metadata-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Image Input","text":"load_raw_metadata(path; decoder::RawDecoder = LibRawDecoder()) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe EXIF fields of a camera RAW file, as RawMosaic's metadata holds them, without keeping the photosites: LibRawDecoder reads the headers only (no unpack). The typed accessors (exposure_settings, …) take it as they take a mosaic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.load_raw_metadata"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.load_raw_mosaic-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Image Input","text":"load_raw_mosaic(path; decoder::RawDecoder = LibRawDecoder()) -> RawMosaic\n\nDecode a camera RAW file (any format LibRaw reads: ARW, CR2, NEF, DNG, …) to its RawMosaic. Only flat Bayer sensors (one colour per photosite) are supported. The decoder's package must be loaded: using LibRaw_jll for LibRawDecoder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.load_raw_mosaic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.photosite_colors-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Image Input","text":"photosite_colors(mosaic::RawMosaic) -> Matrix{UInt8}\n\nEach photosite's colour index (LibRaw's: 0 R, 1 G, 2 B, 3 the second G), the size of mosaic: the pattern tiled from the full raw frame's origin, so photosite (row, column) reads pattern[mod(top + row − 1, n) + 1, mod(left + column − 1, n) + 1] with (top, left) = visible_origin .+ window_origin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.photosite_colors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.raw_coordinates-Tuple{Union{LinearRaw, RawMosaic}, Any}","page":"Image Input","text":"raw_coordinates(img, x) -> SVector{2, Float64}\n\nWhere the point x of an image's pixel frame (COLMAP, (column − ½, row − ½) the centre of pixel (row, column); any 2-vector or tuple) lies on the sensor: in the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}, the frame of every calibration of the raw. THE accessor of an image's sensor-pixel coordinates, raw_frame_map(img)(x):\n\na RawMosaic: the identity as decoded, a window's offset added for a window;\na LinearRaw cell image (:green_cells, :bin2): the cell grid's map, x ↦ 2x (and a window's offset) — a detection on the green cells reports its position through it, never as raw pixels unmapped (half the scale); a position on another plane of a :bin2 image is bayer_plane_map(L, c)(x);\na :demosaic image is refused (ArgumentError), and a plain matrix, which states no frame, has no method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.raw_coordinates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.raw_frame_map-Tuple{LinearRaw}","page":"Image Input","text":"raw_frame_map(L::LinearRaw) -> SimilarityMap{2, …, BayerCellFrame{:G}, PixelFrame{:colmap}}\n\nTHE map of the image's pixel frame (the cell grid, which is its green plane's frame) onto the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap} (the frame of RawMosaic.raw and of every calibration of the raw): x ↦ 2x, exactly, for every Bayer phase and either parity of the visible origin (the cells start at the visible area's first photosite; an odd margin changes only the cell's colour phase); for an image made from a window of the mosaic, x ↦ 2x then the window's offset. Its inverse is inv(raw_frame_map(L)), and a calibration matrix moves with it through Core, reframed_calibration(raw_frame_map(L), K_cells), K_cells in the cell image's pixels (BayerCellFrame{:G}), giving K in the raw's pixels (and back with the inverse; K carries no frame label, so which frame it is in is the caller's statement). A :demosaic image is refused (ArgumentError): it is for display.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.raw_frame_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.raw_frame_map-Tuple{RawMosaic}","page":"Image Input","text":"raw_frame_map(m::RawMosaic) -> SimilarityMap{2, …, PixelFrame{:colmap}, PixelFrame{:colmap}}\n\nThe map of the mosaic's pixels onto the visible raw's PixelFrame{:colmap}: the identity as decoded, a window's offset (x ↦ x + (columns, rows) of its window_origin) for a window.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.raw_frame_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.raw_provenance-Tuple{LinearRaw}","page":"Image Input","text":"raw_provenance(L::LinearRaw) -> NamedTuple\n\nHow the image was made, as plain data a result records: decoder, libraw_version (from the mosaic's metadata; nothing where it states none), mode, gain and frame_map ((; from, to, scale, translation) of raw_frame_map, nothing for :demosaic).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.raw_provenance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.StabilizedImage","page":"Image Input","text":"StabilizedImage(image, saturated, white)\n\nA detection image whose noise has σ = 1 at every pixel: image (a Matrix{Float64}, in noise σ), the saturated pixels (a BitMatrix, masked: asift_detect returns no detection over them) and white, the image's value at the white level (the scale that maps it into [0, 1]). An image that is a Matrix{Float64} and a saturated that is a BitMatrix are held, not copied. variance_stabilize makes one of a RAW's green cells.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.StabilizedImage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detect-Tuple{StabilizedImage}","page":"Image Input","text":"asift_detect(s::StabilizedImage; noise_sigmas = 3.0, kwargs...)\n\nASIFT on a variance-stabilized image (variance_stabilize), its DoG contrast threshold noise_sigmas noise σ: on unit iid noise, the DoG's noise σ in its noisiest searched slice (the finest octave's finest interior slice, the extremum search's first) is derived from the pyramid's own impulse response, and an extremum must exceed noise_sigmas times it. Every coarser slice, and every tilted view, has less noise, so is held to more than noise_sigmas σ of its own. The default 3 is Lowe's three-sigma reading of a contrast: on pure noise, extrema above it are rare.\n\nThe saturated pixels are the exclusion_mask (asift_detect's rule: a detection whose centre lies within three of its scales, the DoG's support, of a masked pixel is dropped): a clipped value is no measurement. A caller's exclusion_mask joins them. The image is scaled by 1 / s.white into [0, 1] for the detector (the warps' fill is mid-grey), the threshold with it. kwargs are asift_detect's but intensity_thr (the threshold is noise_sigmas).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.variance_stabilize-Tuple{LinearRaw}","page":"Image Input","text":"variance_stabilize(L::LinearRaw; electrons_per_dn, read_noise) -> StabilizedImage\n\nThe generalized Anscombe transform of the green cells of L (:green_cells, or :bin2's G plane, the same values) for Poisson–Gaussian noise: after it the noise has σ ≈ 1 at every level, from a few electrons to the white level.\n\nelectrons_per_dn — the sensor's conversion gain at the frame's ISO, electrons per raw code (> 0).\nread_noise — the read noise in raw codes (≥ 0), the codes' rounding included.\n\nNeither is in a RAW file's metadata (LibRaw reports neither): they are the camera's, at the frame's ISO, measured once (e.g. from the variance of the two greens' difference against their mean on a flat scene). The code spans are the photosites' own (L.geometry.green_dn, each green's white − black), and L.gain scales the photon and the read noise alike. A green cell whose value is x = (x₁ + x₂)/2 is α·P + N with α = gain/(4K)·(1/d₁ + 1/d₂), σ² = gain²σ_r²/4·(1/d₁² + 1/d₂²), and its transform is (2/α)·√(max(α x + 3α²/8 + σ², 0)).\n\nL.saturated is carried over: asift_detect masks those pixels rather than reading the clipped values. The transform is pixel-wise, so the detections' positions are the cell grid's, as on any rendering of L (a display rendering for a descriptor among them).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.variance_stabilize"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gray_f32-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:ColorTypes.Gray{<:AbstractFloat}}}","page":"Image Input","text":"gray_f32(img) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nimg as float gray: a float Gray matrix or a bare Float32 matrix (a device array already in the kernels' form) is returned UNTOUCHED — no copy, no wrap, so a CuArray{Float32} stays on the device; anything else (fixed-point gray, any colorant) converts to Gray{Float32}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gray_f32"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gray_u8-Tuple{Matrix{UInt8}}","page":"Image Input","text":"gray_u8(img) -> Matrix{UInt8}\n\nimg as H×W gray bytes, in a Matrix{UInt8} the caller owns. A Matrix{UInt8} is returned as is; a Gray{N0f8} matrix is copied byte for byte (its pixels already are bytes); everything else is quantised through Gray{N0f8} (float colorants LOSE precision here by design — use gray_f32 where sub-pixel photometry matters).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gray_u8"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.box_downsample-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T, N}, Integer}} where {T<:Union{Real, ColorTypes.Colorant}, N}","page":"Image Input","text":"box_downsample(img::AbstractMatrix, f::Integer) -> Matrix\nbox_downsample(img::AbstractArray{T, 3}, f::Integer) -> Array{float(T), 3}\n\nThe H × W image (or H × W × C, per channel) averaged over f × f blocks: output pixel (i, j) is the mean of input rows (i − 1)f + 1 : if and columns (j − 1)f + 1 : jf. The output is H ÷ f × W ÷ f (× C); rows and columns past the last whole block are dropped. The element type is a real number or a colour (Gray{Float32}, RGB{N0f8}, …); the sums accumulate, and the output is stored, in its floating-point type float(T) — Float64 for a Float64 or integer image, Float32 for a Float32 or 8-bit one (the patches BlobBoards' descriptor reads, byte for byte) — each block summed column by column and scaled once by 1/f² in that type. f = 1 returns a float(T) copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.box_downsample"},{"category":"function","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gaussian_blur","page":"Image Input","text":"gaussian_blur(img::AbstractArray{<:Real, 3}, σ[, mask]) -> Array{Float64, 3}\n\nThe H × W × C image convolved per channel with the separable Gaussian of standard deviation σ pixels, truncated at ⌈3σ⌉ pixels and normalized to unit sum; at the image border the kernel is renormalized over the pixels inside it (no padding value enters). The taps are the package's one Gaussian taps builder's, at truncation 3 — not ASIFT's pyramid's 4σ with a mirrored border: the vignetting chain this smooths for was reproduced with this truncation and border rule, so they stay as they are.\n\nWith a mask (an H × W array of weights, 0 outside the support — a fisheye's image circle) it is the NORMALIZED convolution blur(img·mask) / blur(mask): pixels outside the mask do not bleed in, and a pixel where the mask is zero is 0. σ = 0 returns a Float64 copy (masked to zero outside the mask). Edge-safe smoothing of a linear image before it is sampled (the rooftop vignetting chain samples it at fixed points).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gaussian_blur"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.linear_to_srgb-Tuple{Real}","page":"Image Input","text":"linear_to_srgb(x) -> encoded value\n\nThe sRGB (IEC 61966-2-1) opto-electronic transfer function, the inverse of srgb_to_linear: a linear value x ∈ [0, 1] to its display encoding, 12.92x for x ≤ 0.0031308, otherwise 1.055x^(1/2.4) − 0.055, in x's floating-point type (a Float32 image encodes in Float32). The stack's one encoder: a display image is srgb_image.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.linear_to_srgb"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.load_linear_image-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Image Input","text":"load_linear_image(path; srgb = false, downsample = 1) -> Array{Float64, 3}\n\nA PNG file as an H × W × 3 Float64 array of linear RGB values in [0, 1]: the samples AS STORED (read with gamma = 1, so a gAMA/sRGB chunk never re-encodes them; gray files are replicated to three channels, an alpha channel is dropped, 8- and 16-bit depths both normalized to [0, 1]).\n\nsrgb = true: the file is display-encoded (a phone or camera photograph); srgb_to_linear decodes every sample.\ndownsample = f: the decoded image averaged over f × f blocks (box_downsample), in linear space.\n\nThe array is indexed [row, column, channel]; pixel (row, column) is the image's PixelFrame{:julia} pixel (column, row).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.load_linear_image"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.srgb_image-Tuple{AbstractArray}","page":"Image Input","text":"srgb_image([T = N0f8], img; exposure = 1, white_balance = nothing, background = 0, tone = nothing)\n    -> Matrix{RGB{T}} or Matrix{Gray{T}}\n\nA linear image for display, the stack's one display encoding: an H × W × 3 image to RGB{T}, an H × W (or H × W × 1) one to Gray{T}, T a fixed-point type (N0f8, N0f16). Each value is multiplied by the exposure and by its plane's white_balance factor (one per plane, nothing: none), then mapped by the tone curve (a function of a linear value, nothing: none), clamped to [0, 1], encoded by linear_to_srgb and rounded to T. A NaN value (no measurement: a panorama pixel no frame sees) is the background, a display-linear value in [0, 1], encoded alike. The exposure and the factors are positive and finite (ArgumentError otherwise).\n\nRuns where img lives (a broadcast: a device array is encoded on the device; tone must then be kernel-lawful).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.srgb_image"},{"category":"method","location":"api/image/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.srgb_to_linear-Tuple{Real}","page":"Image Input","text":"srgb_to_linear(x) -> linear value\n\nThe sRGB (IEC 61966-2-1) electro-optical transfer function: an encoded value x ∈ [0, 1] to linear, x / 12.92 for x ≤ 0.04045, otherwise ((x + 0.055) / 1.055)^2.4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.srgb_to_linear"},{"category":"section","location":"#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.jl","page":"Home","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures detects, describes and matches image features: the ASIFT detector on the CPU or a GPU, nearest-neighbour descriptor matching, and the learned LoMa descriptors and matchers. The geometric types it returns (feature frames, correspondences, covariances) are VisualGeometryToolkitCore's; this package adds the detectors, the descriptors and the matchers that work on them.","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-it-provides","page":"Home","text":"Detection. ASIFT (IMAS-25) on KernelAbstractions: asift_detect runs on the CPU or any KernelAbstractions GPU backend and returns Detections (a feature frame plus the detector's metadata), optionally with each keypoint's covariance (FeatureCovariance). Beside it: MSER (detect_mser) and the Förstner operator (detect_foerstner). See Detection and The Förstner operator.\nImage input. The grayscale ladders (gray_f32, gray_u8), a PNG as linear radiance (load_linear_image) and a camera RAW file as exact linear values (load_raw).\nMatching. Nearest-neighbour descriptor matching with one KernelAbstractions implementation for the CPU and every GPU backend (nn_match, match), and a stacked matcher over many galleries (nn_match_stacked). See Matching.\nLoMa. The learned DeDoDe descriptors and LoMa matchers, run from the authors' ONNX exports through ONNX Runtime on the CPU or CUDA (LoMaDescriptor, LoMaMatcher).\nExtensions. JLD2 backs FileCache, and Makie draws detections and correspondences (detections!) and holds the one image viewer, linked axes over a menu of image pairs (compare_images).\n\nDetectors return pixel coordinates in the COLMAP convention (the top-left pixel's centre is (0.5, 0.5)); the matchers work in descriptor space and return index matches, never coordinates.","title":"What it provides"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"pkg> add VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\nJulia 1.12 or later; every dependency is in the General registry. Add VisualGeometryToolkitCore as well when you construct frames or points yourself.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-first-example","page":"Home","text":"The example detects the blobs of a synthetic image and matches two sets of descriptors (the README's example; the test suite runs both).\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n# Random is a standard library, shipped with Julia.\nusing Random\n\n# A 120×160 gray image with three Gaussian blobs, given as\n# (row, column, σ, amplitude): two light blobs and one dark one.\nblobs = [(40, 50, 4.0, 0.35), (70, 100, 3.0, -0.3), (30, 130, 6.0, 0.3)]\nimg = [0.5 + sum(a * exp(-((r - y)^2 + (c - x)^2) / (2s^2)) for (y, x, s, a) in blobs)\n       for r in 1:120, c in 1:160]\n\n# Detect with the default IMAS-25 tilt schedule.\ndets = asift_detect(img)\nlength(dets) == 3                      # true\ncount(is_dark, dets) == 1              # true\n\n# A centre is (x, y) in COLMAP pixel coordinates, where the top-left pixel's\n# centre is (0.5, 0.5): the blob at row 70, column 100 is found at (99.5, 69.5).\ndark = only(filter(is_dark, dets))\nround.(dark.center; digits = 1) == [99.5, 69.5]   # true\n\n# Match descriptors, one per row. Here they are random unit vectors, and the\n# second set is the first shuffled and perturbed.\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\nunit_rows(D) = D ./ sqrt.(sum(abs2, D; dims = 2))\nD1 = unit_rows(randn(rng, Float32, 100, 32))\nperm = randperm(rng, 100)\nD2 = unit_rows(D1[perm, :] .+ 0.05f0 .* randn(rng, Float32, 100, 32))\nmatches = nn_match(D1, D2)             # mutual nearest neighbours, cosine similarity\nall(perm[m.second] == m.first for m in matches)   # true","title":"A first example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"Start here. Getting Started walks through the example one step at a time.\n\nHow to. Detect features and read their coordinates, accuracy and covariance (Detection); find corners and circular points (The Förstner operator); match descriptors on the CPU, a GPU, over stacked galleries or with LoMa (Matching).\n\nExplanation. The stack's shared conventions (function names, argument order, coordinate frames and pixel origins) are VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Conventions; Developer Notes records the accuracy bounds other packages rely on.\n\nReference. The API Reference pages (Detection, Image Input, Matching, Cache and Visualization) document every public name; References lists the literature.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Role-in-the-VisualGeometryToolkit-stack","page":"Home","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures builds on VisualGeometryToolkitCore. VisualGeometryToolkitIO writes and reads detections as JSON (json_value, from_json), and VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation fits models to the matches. All toolkit documentation is aggregated at the portal: https://visualgeometrytoolkit.github.io.","title":"Role in the VisualGeometryToolkit stack"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing","page":"Home","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures is part of VisualGeometryToolkit. Cite the toolkit; its CITATION.cff is the source of this entry (GitHub's \"Cite this repository\" button exports it):\n\n@software{pritts2026vgtk,\n  author = {Pritts, James and Sauer, Hendrik},\n  title  = {{VisualGeometryToolkit.jl}: Julia Packages for Multiple-View Geometry},\n  url    = {https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/VisualGeometryToolkit.jl},\n  doi    = {10.5281/zenodo.23548372},\n  year   = {2026}\n}\n\nCite also the papers behind the methods you use, listed on the portal.","title":"Citing"},{"category":"section","location":"#LoMa","page":"Home","text":"LoMaDescriptor and LoMaMatcher run the networks of LoMa by David Nordström, Johan Edstedt, Georg Bökman, Jonathan Astermark, Anders Heyden, Viktor Larsson, Mårten Wadenbäck, Michael Felsberg and Fredrik Kahl. The architectures, the trained weights and the ONNX exports are theirs, downloaded from the authors' release; this package only feeds them keypoints and reads their matches back. The descriptors are DeDoDe's (Edstedt et al.). If you use them in published work, cite both:\n\n@inproceedings{nordstrom2026loma,\n  title     = {{LoMa: Local Feature Matching Revisited}},\n  author    = {David Nordström and Johan Edstedt and Georg Bökman and Jonathan Astermark and Anders Heyden and Viktor Larsson and Mårten Wadenbäck and Michael Felsberg and Fredrik Kahl},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV)},\n  year      = {2026}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{edstedt2024dedode,\n  title     = {{DeDoDe: Detect, Don't Describe --- Describe, Don't Detect for Local Feature Matching}},\n  author    = {Johan Edstedt and Georg Bökman and Mårten Wadenbäck and Michael Felsberg},\n  booktitle = {2024 International Conference on 3D Vision (3DV)},\n  year      = {2024},\n  organization = {IEEE}\n}\n\nLoMa's code is MIT-licensed except its matcher, which inherits LightGlue's Apache-2.0; DeDoDe is MIT except the DINOv2 backbone of DeDoDe-G (Apache-2.0).","title":"LoMa"}]
}
